[{"annotator": "", "document": ["Lactose-free (LÀ) formulas are recommended for infants with conditions affecting lactose digestion. Cows' milk protein-based formulas containing other carbohydrate sources are most often used for such infants. This study compared fat absorption and absorption and retention of nitrogen, calcium, phosphorus, and magnesium in term infants fed either a LÀ or standard lactosecontaining (L) bovine milk protein-based formula. Data from three single-centre, double-blind, randomized, parallel-group metabolic balance studies were combined. After a 4-7-d equilibration period on either LÀ or L formula, a 72-h balance study was performed. Twenty infants received LÀ and 21 received L formula. Besides the LÀ group having a higher percentage of males (65%) and the L group a higher percentage of females (52.4%), other baseline measurements were similar. The majority of nutrient balance data was similar between the two groups. Exceptions were relative nitrogen absorption, calcium intake and calcium retention, magnesium retention, and faecal phosphorus excretion, all of which were significantly higher in the LÀ group. Vitamin D supplementation did not significantly affect either calcium or phosphorus data. This new LÀ formula provided similar nutrients and is a suitable alternative to a L formula in term infants requiring LÀ feedings.", "Lactose, the major mammalian milk carbohydrate, is cleaved by lactase into glucose and galactose, which are then absorbed. During gestation, lactase activity progressively increases, reaching 70% of that of a full-term infant by 35-38 wk (1) . Premature infants, and infants with conditions such as congenital lactase deficiency or secondary lactose intolerance, may require formulas with lower lactose levels. Congenital lactase deficiency is a very rare disorder in which lactase activity is either decreased or absent, while secondary lactose intolerance is frequently associated with either acute or chronic gastroenteritis (2) . In said infections, pathogens may cause significant small intestinal mucosal histological damage (1, 3) , which may be associated with severe brush border damage and substantially decreased lactase levels (1, 3) . Thus, there may be an important role for non-lactose-containing formulas in the treatment of premature infants and those with lactose intolerance. This study's objective was to compare the fat absorption, plus the absorption and retention of nitrogen, calcium, phosphorus and magnesium, in term infants fed either a lactose-free (LÀ) or a lactosecontaining (L) bovine milk protein-based formula.", "Each study was approved by an Institutional Review Board or its equivalent. Parents or guardians gave written informed consent before study enrolment.", "Eligible infants were healthy, exclusively formula-fed, full-term, and, at the time of enrolment, between 2 wk and 6 mo of age and between the 5th and 95th percentiles for weight and length for age (National Center for Health Statistics growth charts) (4).", "Infants were excluded if they had recently had acute gastroenteritis, received corticosteroids within 2 wk of enrolment, had major congenital anomalies preventing normal feedings or frequent and/or abnormal bowel movements consistent with diarrhoea.", "Infants were withdrawn from the study if they developed an acute illness, had a formula intake of `95 kcal/kg/d, or required medication.", "Both formulas were supplied as a powder which, when reconstituted, provided 20 kcal/oz. and identical nutrient levels, with the exception of higher calcium and phosphorus levels in the LÀ formula, and different carbohydrate sources. The LÀ formula contained 75% maltodextrin and 25% sucrose, while the L formula contained 100% lactose. Wyeth Nutritionals International (WNI) performed the analyses of the formulas for fat, nitrogen, calcium, phosphorus, and magnesium content, which were used to calculate the nutrient intake. Each of the two study formulas had a unique code number that was blinded to both the investigators and the infants' parents or guardians.", "The L formula (SMA/S-26), and the LÀ formula (SMA LF/S-26 LF) were provided by WNI (Philadelphia, PA, USA).", "The data from three single-centre, double-blind, randomized, in-patient, parallel-group trials were combined for this report. Infants were assigned to receive one of the two study formulas, which had essentially identical smell and taste, according to a randomization schedule. Infants were to receive no medications, including vitamin or mineral supplements, or beikost during the study. A regular schedule of monitoring visits, protocol compliance and source document verification of the data in the case report forms was conducted by an independent clinical research organization (CRO) (Pharmaceutical Research Associates, Inc., Charlottesville, VA, USA).", "The study was composed of two periods, an Equilibration Period (EQP) and a Metabolic Balance Period (MBP). During the 4-7 consecutive day EQP, the infant was exclusively fed one of the study formulas ad libitum. On EQP Day 1, a history and physical examination including weight, length and head circumference was performed. Weighing was also repeated at the MBP start and finish.", "The 72-h MBP immediately followed the EQP, during which a research assistant accompanied the infant at all times. Exclusive ad libitum feedings with the assigned study formula were continued during the MBP. Study formula intake was precisely measured, and spilled or regurgitated formula was quantified and subtracted from the formula intake to determine the net formula intake. The MBP began with the administration of study formula containing an external marker, charcoal or carmine-red. The appearance of the first marked stool prompted the beginning of stool collection.", "Seventy-two hours after the first marked formula was fed, the marker was again administered in the formula. At this point, the study formulas were discontinued, and the infants were fed a commercially available L bovine milk-based formula. Appearance of the second marked stool signalled the end of the MBP. The first marked stool and all subsequent stools up to, but not including, the second marked stool were collected and refrigerated. During the 24-h period after administration of the second marked formula, stool collection was completed and a physical examination was performed. Urine collection began when the first marked formula was fed and ended 72 h thereafter. All urine was refrigerated until analysed.", "Each infant's pooled stool (which was homogenized) and pooled urine was analysed for nitrogen (at two sites only), calcium, phosphorus, and magnesium content (5, 6) . Stools were also analysed for fat content (7, 8) .", "The process of determining the amount of each nutrient in the infants' urine and faeces varied slightly among the three sites, so the data were standardized before the excretion, absorption and retention variables were calculated. For example, at one site, faecal testing occurred using a representative sample of the total collection, and values for nutrient excretion, absorption, and retention were adjusted by a factor equal to the ratio of the total amount of faeces to the amount of faeces analysed.", "Categorical variables were summarized using n, frequency and percent. Continuous variables were summarized using n, mean, standard deviation, and a 95% confidence interval around the mean. Version 6.08 of SAS 1 was used to provide all data summaries, statistical analyses and data listings (9) (10) (11) (12) .", "No attempt was made to impute values for missing data. Infants with missing values for a particular variable in a particular analysis were not included in that analysis. The level of significance was = 0.05 for all analyses. No adjustments were made to the significance levels because of multiple comparisons.", "The number and percent of infants enrolled in the study by each investigator were summarized by formula group and gender. The number and percent of infants who entered the study, completed the study, and discontinued the study (categorized according to the reason for discontinuation) were summarized by formula group.", "Summary statistics are presented for gender, gestational age at birth, birth length, weight and head circumference. This summary is presented by formula group and for both groups combined. The study was not performed to evaluate growth, but rather to determine absorption, retention and excretion of various minerals. Thus, the study did not include a follow-up duration or have the statistical power to detect important differences in such outcomes.", "Definitions are as follows:", "Nutrient Intake (g or mg/d) = (mean formula intake per day during balance period) Â (study formula nutrient concentration) Nutrient Intake during (g or mg/kg/d) = (mean formula intake per kg mean body weight per day) Nutrient Excretion = (nutrient concentration in faeces or urine) Â (mean faeces or urine) In Urine or Faeces (g or mg/kg/d) = (output per day during balance period) Ä mean weight during balance period Nutrient Absorption (g or mg/kg/d) = nutrient intake À faecal nutrient excretion Nutrient Retention = (nutrient intake) À (faecal nutrient excretion urine nutrient excretion) Formula, study site, gender, age and several interaction terms were included in the regression model as independent predictors. If significant interactions were found, summary statistics were provided to describe the interaction. Exploratory analyses of the calcium and phosphorus balance variables were performed by repeating the analyses described above on the subset of infants who did not receive vitamin D. Adverse events were presented as summary statistics and were organized by body system.", "Forty-one infants, 20 receiving LÀ formula and 21 L formula, were enrolled (Table 1 ). Forty (97.6%) completed the study. Twenty-three in the study were male, and a greater percentage of males (65.0%) received LÀ formula (Table 1 ). All mean values were similar except for weight change from baseline to the end of the balance period, which was higher for infants who received LÀ formula (375.8 g) than for those who received L formula (298.8 g) (Table 1 ).", "Mean formula intake for the LÀ and L groups was 861.1 ml/d and 843.6 ml/d, respectively. Mean normalized formula intake was 181.5 ml/kg/d for the LÀ formula group compared with 177.4 ml/kg/d for the L formula group.", "Except as noted, the nutrient analyses detailed below were from the 40 infants who completed the balance study. No significant differences in fat balance data were found (Table 2 ).", "No significant differences in nitrogen balance data were found (Table 3 ). Infants from one site did not have nitrogen values because the specimens for nitrogen were inadvertently not analysed.", "No significant differences in calcium balance data were documented except for calcium intake (p = 0.046) and normalized calcium intake (p = 0.014), which was higher in the infants receiving the LÀ formula (Table 3 ).", "No significant differences in phosphorus balance data were found except for faecal phosphorus excretion, which was significantly higher in the infants who received the LÀ formula (p = 0.022) (Table 3 ).", "No significant differences in magnesium balance data were found except that infants who received LÀ formula had significantly higher mean magnesium retention (p = 0.049) (Table 3 ). In these analyses, three infants had negative magnesium absorption data and were not included in the overall analyses.", "Additional vitamin D was not to be administered during the study; however, 10 infants at one site received vitamin D inadvertently. We therefore performed additional analyses of the calcium and phosphorus data for the subset of infants who did not receive vitamin D. No significant differences between formula groups were found in these exploratory analyses for urinary or faecal excretion or absorption. Mean and normalized intake and retention in infants who received LÀ formula were significantly higher (p = 0.043, p = 0.027, and p = 0.015) (Table 3 ). No other concomitant medications were given during the study. ", "One infant receiving the LÀ formula was withdrawn after experiencing severe diarrhoea; two infants receiving the L formula had vomiting, while another had irritability.", "Our trial was of a classic metabolic balance design and was, therefore, not intended as one in which weight gain would be assessed; therefore, the overall weight gain and weight change (g/kg/d) in the infants who received the LÀ formula cannot be assessed as being truly valid until it is confirmed in a properly powered multiclinic growth study of at least 3 mo duration. At the present time, whether this new LÀ formula actually is associated with any increase in weight gain compared to a L formula is only speculative and requires confirmation. Our LÀ group had significantly higher calcium intake, normalized intake, and calcium retention, which may be particularly important in clinical situations where calcium requirements are high, such as in the premature infant, where there is a great risk for the development of osteopenia and rickets (13, 14) . Despite this, only a few studies have investigated the relationship between the carbohydrate an infant consumes and calcium absorption. In one such study, no significant difference in calcium absorption was found between premature infants fed formula with 100% lactose or with 50% glucose polymers and 50% lactose (15) . In other studies, including the original observation by Tucker et al., infants fed 100% lactose as the carbohydrate source had greater calcium absorption than infants fed formula containing polycose (a cornstarch hydrolysate) or a combination of polycose and sucrose (16) (17) (18) . More recently, however, a study of premature infants demonstrated enhanced calcium absorption in infants fed formula containing glucose polymers compared with those fed L (19) . In that study, the authors felt that the glucose polymers were more easily digested than either the lactose or the cornstarch (polycose) preparations used in earlier studies. This observation is supported by a study in which glucose polymers were found to be more easily digested and absorbed by premature infants than lactose (20) . In our study, the higher calcium concentrations in the LÀ formula may have resulted in the higher intake values for infants receiving this formula. Although urinary and faecal calcium excretion and calcium absorption values were not significantly different, calcium retention was significantly greater than for those infants receiving L formula, suggesting that calcium absorption was not impaired because lactose was not the carbohydrate in the LÀ formula. The LÀ formula has a calcium intake that was greater than the formula used in the L group, which probably explains why the former group had a higher retention level.", "Faecal phosphorus excretion was significantly higher in infants receiving LÀ formula than in those receiving L formula, although no significant differences in phosphorus intake or phosphorus absorption were found between the two groups. We believe that, despite there being no statistically significant differences in phosphorus intake between the study groups, the increased phosphorus content of the LÀ formula may nevertheless be contributing to this difference in faecal phosphorus excretion.", "More magnesium was retained by infants receiving the LÀ formula than by those receiving the L formula, although both formulas had identical magnesium target levels (p = 0.0486). No other significant differences in magnesium balance results between the formula groups were found. The difference in magnesium retention may have been due to a single infant with an unusually high magnesium retention value of 10.47 mg/kg/d. It is also important to note that the relative retention was not different between the study groups.", "Both study formulas were well tolerated. Adverse events were limited to one infant receiving LÀ formula who experienced diarrhoea, and three infants receiving L formula who experienced vomiting or irritability.", "In summary, we believe that our balance study data documents that this LÀ formula provides similar nutrients and is a suitable alternative to L formula in infants who require feedings containing no lactose. Clinical trials where weights are the outcome measured would be necessary before recommending widespread use of this formula.", "© Scandinavian University Press 1999. ISSN 0803-5253 Acta Pñdiatr 88: 1211±5. 1999", "ACTA PAEDIATR 88 (1999)"], "domain_risk_levels": ["Some Concerns", "Low Risk", "Low Risk", "Low Risk", "Low Risk"], "overall_risk_level": "Some Concerns", "paper_id": "29308", "pdf_hash": "23c81053d1af5b6728de540b63826954835ecedb", "result": [{"addedParagraphs": [], "ctxP1Score": "0.260", "ctxP2Score": "0.252", "ctxP3Score": "0.243", "ctxParagraph1": "Infants were withdrawn from the study if they developed an acute illness, had a formula intake of `95 kcal/kg/d, or required medication.", "ctxParagraph1Feedback": null, "ctxParagraph2": "Each study was approved by an Institutional Review Board or its equivalent. Parents or guardians gave written informed consent before study enrolment.", "ctxParagraph2Feedback": null, "ctxParagraph3": "The data from three single-centre, double-blind, randomized, in-patient, parallel-group trials were combined for this report. Infants were assigned to receive one of the two study formulas, which had essentially identical smell and taste, according to a randomization schedule. Infants were to receive no medications, including vitamin or mineral supplements, or beikost during the study. A regular schedule of monitoring visits, protocol compliance and source document verification of the data in the case report forms was conducted by an independent clinical research organization (CRO) (Pharm