[{"annotator": "", "document": ["The Neonatal Inhaled Nitric O, vl~)e Study Group ':' Background: Inhaled nitric oxide (INO) improved oxygenation and reduced the occurrence of death or extracorporeal membrane oxygenation in term and near-term hypoxic neonates. We report the results of neurodevelopmental follow-up of infants enrolled in the NINOS trial.", "Hypoxic infants >34 weeks' gestation and < 14 days of age were randomized to 20 ppm INO or 100% oxygen as control. Comprehensive neurodevelopmental assessment of survivors occurred at 18 to 24 months of age. Results: A total of 235 infants were enrolled in the original trial. There were 36 deaths, 20 of 121 infants in the control group and 16 of 114 infants in the INO-treated group. Of the 199 surviving infants, 175 (86.9%) were seen for follow-up (88 members of the control group and 85 members of the INO-treated group), and 135 infants were normal (69 [79.3%] members of the control group and 66 [77.6%] members of the INO-treated group). Twenty-two infants had sensorineural hearing loss (12 members of the control group and 10 members of the INO-treated group). Moderate to severe cerebral pals,/occurred in 13 infants (7 infants in the control group and 6 infants in the INO-treated group). Mental developmental index scores (87 _+ 18.7 in the control group vs 85 _+ 21.7 in the INO-treated group) and psychomotor developmental index scores (93.6 _+ 17.5 in the control group vs 85.7 _+ 21.2 in the INO-treated group) were not different. A total of 29.6% of the control group compared with 34.5% of the INO-treated group had at least one disability. Infants with congenital diaphragmatic hernia, enrolled in a separate but parallel trial, had similar outcomes with a higher incidence of sensorineural hearing loss. Conclusion: Inhaled nitric oxide is not associated with an increase in neurodevelopmental, behavioral, or medical abnormalities at 2 years of age. (J Pediatr 2000; 136:611-7) Dr Finer, as co-principal investigator of the study, assumes responsibility for the overall content and integrity of the article. *The members of The Neonatal Inhaled Nitric Oxide Study Group are listed in the Appendix.", "Inhaled nitric oxide is an effective selective puhnonary vasodilator that can improve oxygenation in a variety of disorders. The mechanism of action involves the production of cyclic guanosine monophosphate and subsequent The overall results from these studies have demonstrated that INO can improve oxygenation and reduce the need for extracorporeal membrane oxygenation. 10 Although these results are encouraging, the acceptance of INO as an effective therapy in such infants must be predicated on the safety and long-term benefits. The primary hypothesis of the NINOS trial 5 and a parallel pilot study of infants with congenital diaphragmatic hernla 11 was that the administration of 1NO to infants _>34 weeks with hypoxemic respiratory failure and an oxygen index of _>25 would reduce the risk of death by day 120 or discharge (whichever came first) or the need for ECMO from 50% in the control group to 30% in the INO group, a relative reduction of 40%. A secondary hypothesis for this trial was We report the results of the follow-up of the surviving infants enrolled in these trials from January 1994 to May 1996.", "The study was a prospective doublemasked randomized trial conducted in 18 neonatal units, 9 in the United States and 9 in Canada. The full details of the trial method have been published previously, s", "Any infant >34 weeks by best obstetric estimate who required assisted ventilation for hypoxemic respiratory failure caused by persistent pulmonary hypertension, meconium aspiration, pneumonia/sepsis, respiratory distress syndrome, or suspected pulmona W hypoplasia associated with oligohydramnios and premature rupture of the membranes and had 20Is (mean arterial pressure × FiO 2 x 100)/PaO2] _>25 at least 15 minutes apart was eligible for participation in the trial. Infants with CDH were enrolled in a separate but parallel trial, the results of which have been reported. 11 Parental permission was required before randomization. Each study center obtained institutional review board approval before enrolling infants.", "Infants were ineligible if they were >14 clays of age or had known congenital heart disease, or if a decision had been made not to provide full treatment.", "Infants were stratified by center and randomized by telephone with software developed and managed by the coordinating center to either control (100% oxygen) or INO treatment.", "All surviving infants were scheduled to be seen at 18 to 24 months for a complete history, physical, neurologic examination, Bayley testing, 12 and diagnostic audiologic assessment. Interim medical history and socioeconomic data were collected. The neuro]oglc and developmental evaluations were performed by certified examiners who had been trained to reliability in the examination procedures and were blinded to study group status. Seven infants (1 member of the control group and 6 members of the INO group) were assessed by non-masked Barley administrators. The neurologic evaluation was based on the Amiel-Tison 13 neurologic assessment and included an evaluation of tone, strength, reflexes, and posture. Cerebral palsy was defined as abnormal muscle tone in at least one extremity and abnormal control of movement and posture. Cerebral palsy was then categorized as mild, moderate, or severe. Mild was defined as motor function that interferes slightly with but does not prevent age-appropriate motor activities, it included nonfluent walking, asymmetric walking, or persistent toe walking with tight Achilles tendon (90 ° or greater) resulting from increased tone. Moderate cerebral palsy was defined as impairment of motor function that interfered with age-appropriate motor activities and was associated with ambulation with assistive devices or no ambulation. Severe cerebral palsy includes impairment of function, which interferes with age-appropriate motor activity; the child does not ambulate or sit. The Bayley Scales of Infant Development I112 were administered, a mental developmental index and a psychomotor developmental index were derived, and behavioral ratings were recorded. A comprehensive audiologic assessment included speech awareness in sound field, speech awareness by bone conduction, warbled pure-tone thresholds in sound field at 250 to 4000 Hz, and tympanometry. Responses were compared with previously established norms. 14 For purposes of the study, normal hearing was defined as threshold responses to speech awareness in sound field and pure-tone thresholds in sound field at _<40 dB. Children were categorized into 4 groups: normal hearing, permanent sensorineural hearing loss, conductive loss, or undetermined and requiring further evaluation. A diagnosis of blindness was based on an ophthalmologist's report of uncorrectable vision _<20/200 in the better eye.", "Continuous variables were compared with t tests or Wilcoxon tests, and discrete variables were compared by chi-squared analysis. A P value <.05 was considered significant.", "A total of 235 infants were enrolled in the original trial (Table I ); 36 infants died before discharge: 20 (16.5%) infants in the control group and 16 (14%) infants in the INO-treated group. Of the 199 surviving infants, 173 (86.9%) had at least a partial clinical evaluation at 18 to 24 months: 87 infants in the control group and 85 infants in the INO-treated group. One INO-trcated infant died after initial discharge at 401 days of age, and an additional 26 (14 infants in the control group and 12 infants in the INO-treated group) were not evaluated at follow-up. The 25 children who were lost to follow-up were compared with the remaining 173 infants. Those lost to follow-up were lighter at birth (3174 g + 478 g vs 3493 g + 587 g, P = .003) and were less likely to have received ECMO (24% vs 50%, P = .016).", "Neonatal characteristics including birth weight, gestation, sex, bronchopulmonary dysp/asia, and country of origin did not differ between groups. The 2 study groups had similar findings for maternal marital status, education, household income, medical insurance, and the number of children living with at least one biologic parent. In a similar manner, no differences in the biologic or contributing father's education or occupation were found between the 2 groups.", "Information regarding post-discharge medical morbidities and use of community resources from discharge to the time of assessment was collected by a standardized parental questionnaire with chart confirmation when available (Table II ). By the 18-month evaluation 39.1% of the control group and 32.9% of the INO group had been re-hospitalized at least once. Continued medication use including bronchodilators, diuretics, or anticonvulsants was similar for the 2 groups. Additional medical requirements after discharge for the control and INO groups were as follows: apnea monitor or pulse oximeter (9.2% vs 11.8%), oxygen therapy (16.1% vs 16.5%), and gastric tube feedings (9.2% vs 8.2%).", "Most of the infants were neurologically normal (Table Ill ). Cerebral We also compared the outcomes of infants from the main and CDH trials who did not receive ECMO with those who received ECMO. No differences were found in any of the demographic household variables including maternal marital status, education, household income, and medical history between these groups, and no differences were seen in the neurologic diagnoses as shown in Table II1 between these 2 groups. palsy rates were similar at 10.3% for the control group and 11.8% for the 1NO-treated group. Moderate to severe cerebral palsy occurred in 13 infants: 7 infants in the control group and 6 infants in the INO-treated group. Seizures occurred in 14.9% of the control group and 4.7% of the INO group (P = .046) after initial discharge. Nine infants were found to be visually impaired: 5 infants in the control group and 4 infants in the INO group. Two infants in each group were found to be blind in one or both eves.", "Overall, 157 (90.8%) infants received an audiologic assessment, of whom 110 (70.1%) were normal. Permanent sensorineural hearing loss was identified in 12 (14.6%) infants in the control group and 10 (13.3%, not significant) in the INO-treated group. Seven infants in the control group and 7 infants in the INO group had been fitted with hearing aids at the time of the evaluation. A total of 19 inthnts (9 infants in the control group and 10 infants in the INO group) were classified as undetermined because of suggestive or incomplete findings (Table liD. In addition, 8 infants in the control group had tympanostomy tubes in place at the time of testing compared with 1 infant in the INO-treated group.", "A successful Bayley examination was conducted in 79 infants in the INO group and 75 infants in the control group (Table III ). No differences were found between the 1NO and control groups for their mean Bayley mental and motor scores or the percent of children scoring <70. There were also no differences between the study groups for children who were severely developmentally delayed (Barley MDI or PDI of <50) or for Bayley behavior ratings for orientation, emotional regulation, motor quality, and total score.", "The percent of children who were assessed to have at least one disability (at least one of the following: cerebral palsy, MDI <70, PDI <70, a hearing impairment, or bilateral blindness) was 29.6% for the control group and 34.5% for the INO group (Table III ).", "A parallel arm of the original study enrolled 53 children with congenital diaphragmatic hernia including 25 who received INO and 28 infants in the control group (Table IV ). Thirteen (52%) of 25 infants in the INO group and 16 (57%) of 28 infants in the control group survived. Eight (62%) infants in the 1NO group and 14 (87%) infants in the control group were evaluated with the same follow-up protocol at 20 _+ 4 months. No neurodevelopmental outcome differences were identified between the CDH INO and control groups. A high rate of sensorineural hearing loss was identified in both groups (59% in CDH control", "INO therapy improves oxygenation in the term and near-term neonate, and more important, it reduces the need for ECMO in treated infants. 10 The longer term effects of such treatment have not been well studied. INO treatment should not exert a significant effect on organs apart from the lung, because it is rapidly converted to nitrosohemoglobin and subsequently to methemoglobin, which is rapidly cleared by methemoglobln reductase. However, there has been a recent suggestion that the hemoglobin may transport nltrosohemoglobin to peripheral tissues, where the nitric oxide may be released. 15 Only one report has described the long-term outcome of a group of neonates who were treated with INO for persistent pulmonary hypertension. 16 Fifty-one were evaluated at 1 year of" age, and 33 were evaluated at 2 years. At 1 year 11.8% and at 2 years 12.1% had severe neurodevelopmental disability. The results of this trial demonstrate a similar level of severe neurodeve]opmental disability, with an overall incidence of cerebral palsy of 11.0%, 10.3% for the control group compared with 11.8% for the INO group. Although this trial was not prospectively designed or powered to detect a difference in neurodevelop-mental outcome between infants who required ECMO and those who did not, we nonetheless compared those cohorts and found no difference.", "The treatment of term and near-term hypoxic infants involves a number of as yet unproven therapies including alkalosis, hyperventilation, surfactant, and steroids. Even high-frequency ventilation, which has been demonstrated to be an effective rescue for some infants, 17 has not been evaluated with respect to long-term neurodevelopmental outcome. Our results, however, demonstrate that INO therapy is not associated with an increased incidence of neurodevelopmental impairment when added to current aggressive supportive therapy used in a consistent fashion by 18 centers. It will be important to continue to follow the progress of the infants in this trial as they enter school, not only to determine the incidence of school learning disorders but also to determine whether their performance improves as their underlying physical disabilities resolve.", "The NINOS protocol required that each center adhere to a written protocol of support for the infants enrolled in the trial and specified the use of such therapies as alkalosis, surfactant, and hlgh-frequency ventilation. The results of the trial demonstrated that these therapies were, in fact, used almost identically for the 1NO-treated and control groups. 5 The incidence of neurodevelopmental impairment in our trial is similar to that reported for the 1-year outcome of surviving infants of the U.K. ECMO trial (85 of the 185 randomized infants died). 18 In the U.K. study a lower rate of disability in infants at 1 year was reported for 62 surviving infants who had been allocated to receive ECMO than for the 37 conventionally treated infants; 73% of survivors in each group had no signs of impairment or disability. Our findings were similar: 70..4% of assessed infants in the control group and 65.5% of assessed infants in the INO group had no evidence of neurologic, sensory, or developmental impairment at a mean age of 20 months. The rate of disability in our population was not affected by the requirement for ECMO, suggesting that a significant number of infants with hypoxic respiratory failure may have sustained brain insult before the need for ECMO and perhaps before birth. 19 Our control and INO-treatecl groups were well matched for all measured socioeconomic variables, which are often unreported in interventional therapeutic trials and are important in influencing longer term neurodevelopmental outcome. In addition, the treated and control groups had similar frequencies of hospital readmissions, need for medications, and other associated problems, including the need for tube feedings and home oxygen and monitoring.", "We were also concerned about hearing deficits in this high-risk population, and thus our protocol required detailed audiologic assessment. Overall, we found higher rates of hearing disorders than previously reported, including the diagnosis of both sensorineural and conductive hearing loss. Cheung et al 2° found that a high proportion of infants who survived neonatal ECMO passed the auditory brainstem response in the neonatal period but subsequently had late-onset sensorineural hearing loss. We believe that the incidence of hearing impairment at 18 months in our study may be an underestimate because of the increasing evidence of late-onset hearing loss in children with a history of persistent pulmonary hypertension or EC/ViO. 21"23 We diagnosed an even higher incidence of sensorineural hearing loss in our small CDH trial. This result is consistent with the findings of Robertson et al, 24 whose follow-up assessments of 37 of 38 CDH survivors at 2 to 5 years of age revealed that 60% of the infants had permanent sensorineural hearing loss.", "We conclude that INO, when used according to the protocol of the NINOS study, is not associated with an increase in neurodevelopmental, behavioral, or medical abnormalities at", "to 24 months of age. INO is a beneficial therapy for the hypoxic term and near-term neonate that will reduce the need for ECIVIO without an increase in neurodevelopmental abnormality."], "domain_risk_levels": ["Low Risk", "Low Risk", "Low Risk", "Low Risk", "Some Concerns"], "overall_risk_level": "Some Concerns", "paper_id": "29323", "pdf_hash": "f034cfd96950fd2009fd93ec2e092d499eebebda", "result": [{"addedParagraphs": [], "ctxP1Score": "0.390", "ctxP2Score": "0.330", "ctxP3Score": "0.303", "ctxParagraph1": "Infants were stratified by center and randomized by telephone 