erformed the intubation. All other responses to laryngoscopy and intubation were assessed by an observer who was unaware of the end-tidal anesthetic concentrations. The time from removal of the face mask to completion of tracheal intubation was recorded. If the intubation was unsuccessful, anesthesia was induced intravenously and the lungs were ventilated with oxygen before a second attempt. The incidence of laryngospasm among those who were not intubated on the first attempt was recorded.", "ctxParagraph2Feedback": null, "ctxParagraph3": "Anesthesiology, V 91, No 3, Sep 1999", "ctxParagraph3Feedback": null, "domain_number": 4, "expertExplanation": "No, the measurement or ascertainment of the outcome (successful intubation) was consistent between intervention groups as it was based on objective criteria such as absence of purposeful movement of the extremities, movement of the vocal cords preventing intubation, and coughing or bucking during or immediately after intubation. The criteria were assessed by the anesthesiologist performing the intubation and an observer who was unaware of the end-tidal anesthetic concentrations, ensuring consistency in measurement.", "expertPrediction": "no", "gptExplanation": "No, the measurement or ascertainment of the outcome (successful intubation) was consistent between intervention groups as it was based on objective criteria such as absence of purposeful movement of the extremities, movement of the vocal cords preventing intubation, and coughing or bucking during or immediately after intubation. The criteria were assessed by the anesthesiologist performing the intubation and an observer who was unaware of the end-tidal anesthetic concentrations, ensuring consistency in measurement.", "gptPrediction": "no", "gptResponse": "{
