esponses to tracheal intubation in children.", "ctxParagraph3Feedback": null, "domain_number": 5, "expertExplanation": "all eligible reported results for the outcome measurement correspond to all intended analyses", "expertPrediction": "probably no", "gptExplanation": "The numerical result is likely to have been selected from multiple eligible analyses of the data as the study design permitted prospective randomization of all patients and yielded information about the interaction between independent variables, indicating that various analyses were conducted to determine the nature of the interaction between nitrous oxide and sevoflurane.", "gptPrediction": "yes", "gptResponse": "{
    "prediction": "yes",
    "explanation": "The numerical result is likely to have been selected from multiple eligible analyses of the data as the study design permitted prospective randomization of all patients and yielded information about the interaction between independent variables, indicating that various analyses were conducted to determine the nature of the interaction between nitrous oxide and sevoflurane."
}", "question": "Is the numerical result being assessed likely to have been selected, on the basis of the results,
from multiple eligible analyses of the data?"}]}]

[{"annotator": "", "document": ["The objective of our study was to compare the safety and efficacy of discharging asthmatic children from hospital on three versus four hourly nebulized salbutamol. The setting was a tertiary referral paediatric hospital in Sydney, NSW, Australia. The design was a randomized controlled parallel group study. All children admitted to hospital with acute asthma and who were over 18 months of age were eligible to enter the study. Patients were excluded if they had non-English speaking parents, no telephone, or chronic cardiac or neurological disease. Children were treated according to standard asthma management but were randomly allocated to be discharged on three or four hourly nebulized salbutamol. Patients were surveyed using a telephone questionnaire 1 to 2 weeks after discharge. The primary outcome measure was re-presentation to the Emergency Department (ED) within 7 days. Other outcomes included readmission to hospital, re-presentation to the local doctor, parental satisfaction and length of hospital stay. A total of 63 children were enrolled in the study (32 in the three hourly group and 31 in the four hourly group). There were no re-presentations to the ED or hospital readmissions within 1 to 2 weeks in either group. However, re-presentations to the local doctor were common, 71.8% in the three hourly and 74.1% in the four hourly groups, respectively. These were predominantly for routine review. The mean (± SD) hospital length of stay was not significantly different between the three and four hourly groups, 48.94 (± 20.61) and 54.88 (± 32.59) hours, respectively (P = 0.672). Parents felt the timing of discharge was 'too early' in five (15.6%) of three hourly and five (16.1%) of four hourly patients. Three (9.7%) of the four hourly but none of the three hourly patients felt they were sent home 'later than necessary'. Five (15.1%) of the three hourly and three (9.7%) of the four hourly group parents did not feel comfortable looking after their child at home immediately after discharge. None of these differences were statistically significant. Discharge of asthmatic children from hospital on three hourly nebulized salbutamol is as safe and effective as on four hourly. Parents are generally very satisfied with timing of discharge, irrespective of frequency of nebulization. Earlier discharge benefits both the child and their family, and improves hospital bed utilization.", "Asthma is the most common chronic illness in childhood. At the Royal Alexandra Hospital for Children (RAHC) acute asthma is the most common cause for admission, accounting for nearly 2000 admissions each year. The current (over the last 12 months) average length of stay is 2.3 days. The hospital has an informal policy, based on current guidelines, [1] [2] [3] of discharging asthmatic children once stabilized on four hourly nebulized salbutamol. However, these guidelines are based on clinical consensus, rather than objective evidence. Clinical practice varies from hospital to hospital, and at RAHC many clinicians discharge patients on three hourly nebulized salbutamol. The outcome in children discharged earlier has not been formally assessed nor has it been compared to outcome in those discharged later. This study was designed to evaluate the outcome of earlier discharge to establish sound, evidence-based guidelines for the optimal timing of discharge of asthmatic children from hospital.", "We conducted a randomized controlled parallel group study. All children over 18 months of age admitted to the hospital with acute asthma were eligible to enter the study. Patients were excluded if they had non-English speaking parents, no telephone, or chronic cardiac or neurological disease.", "The Heads of all five Medical Units were contacted and all agreed to be part of the study. Virtually all paediatricians within those Units consented to their patients participating in the study. Apart from the timing of discharge all other asthma management remained as standard for that medical team (see Fig. 1 ).", "Children were randomized within 24 h of admission, after parents had given informed consent for them to enter the study. The two treatment arms were discharge on three hourly and discharge on four hourly nebulized salbutamol. Randomization was achieved using sealed, shuffled opaque envelopes containing prerandomized allocation, blocked into equal 1:1 proportions for each medical team. Patients were randomized by the attending team's resident or registrar, by selecting an envelope from the shuffled pile. The parents and the attending house staff and paediatrician were aware of the group to which their child was randomized. However, the principal investigator (MS), who performed the followup questionnaire, was blinded as to which group the patient belonged.", "As the patient improved, discharge was dictated by the group to which the patient had been allocated. Namely, when they had reached either three or four hourly nebulized salbutamol, and were judged to be clinically stable. The progression to three or four hourly salbutamol was according to the attending team's usual asthma management, based on improvements in the child's condition. The decision that the child was clinically stable and ready for discharge was also at the discretion of the attending medical team and not dictated by any specific criteria set out in the study protocol.", "Following discharge, all parents were interviewed one to two weeks later via a telephone administered questionnaire by MS (Appendix 1). The questionnaire had been previously piloted in 10 sets of parents to ensure no ambiguity of questions.", "For the purposes of sample size calculation we assumed a re-presentation rate (to the hospital Emergency Department) within 7 days of discharge of 10% in the four hourly discharge group. This assumption was based on both adult studies examining the effect of a short course of prednisolone in the prevention of early relapse after the emergency room treatment of acute asthma, 4, 5 and on paediatric studies of preschool children suffering from repeated asthma attacks related to upper respiratory tract infections and given preventive corticosteroids. 6 In order to ensure an 80% power to detect a 20% difference in representation rate between the two groups (i.e. 10 vs 30% re-presentation rate), 70 patients were required in each group, with an alpha error of 5%. Allowing for a 20% drop-out rate/incomplete data, we calculated that a total of 160 patients were needed to complete the study.", "A number of outcomes were assessed and compared between the two groups. These included re-presentation to either a local doctor or the ED or hospital readmission within one week of discharge. Parental concerns about the timing of discharge ('too early' or 'too late') and parental comfort plus satisfaction were analysed. Other variables analysed were the number of days following on which the patient required salbutamol, and the number of days until the parents thought their child was 'back to normal'. The length of hospital stay was also compared between the two groups.", "Statistical analysis was performed using Chi-squared tests for dichotomous variables (the timing of discharge, parental comfort and parental satisfaction) between the two groups. A Mann-Whitney U-test compared the median age, days after discharge until back to normal, and days after discharge that salbutamol was required for, between the two groups. An unpaired Student's t-test was used to compare the mean length of stay between the three and four hourly groups. A P value < 0.05 was considered statistically significant.", "We enrolled 63 children in the study, 32 in the three hourly group and 31 in the four hourly group. The mean (Ϯ SD) age in the three hourly group was 5.06 (Ϯ 2.98) years, and in the four hourly group 5.07 (Ϯ 3.21) years (P = 0.951). The male to female ratio was 1.13 and 2.87 in the three and four hourly groups, respectively (P = 0.004). All parents were contacted within 2 weeks of discharge and none were lost to follow-up. Based on the parents' answers in the questionnaire, all children in both three and four hourly groups were discharged according to the group to which they had been allocated.", "No patients in the study were either readmitted to hospital or attended an ED within 1 week of discharge. During the study it was clear that our sample size calculation, which assumed a 10% re-presentation rate to the ED, was incorrect. Thus the study was terminated after the enrolment of 63 patients. Other outcomes are compared in Table 1 .", "Re-presentation to the local doctor occurred in 71.8% and 74.1% of three and four hourly groups, respectively, within 1 week of discharge (P = 1.000). Of the three hourly group, 86% re-presented to their local doctor not because of parental concern primarily but because the hospital doctors had recommended this. In the four hourly group the figure was 69%.", "The timing of discharge was felt by parents to be 'too early' in five (15.6%) of three hourly and five (16.1%) of four hourly patients (P = 1.0000). Three (9.7%) of the four hourly group felt they were sent home 'too late', vs 0% in the three hourly group (P = 0.113, Fischer's exact two-tailed test). However, 12 (37.5%) of the three hourly group and eight (25.8%) of the four hourly group were concerned, in general terms, about the timing of their child's discharge from hospital. The parents level of comfort of caring for their child at home immediately following discharge was assessed via a scoring system, ranging from one to five (very uncomfortable to very comfortable). Five (15.1%) of the three hourly group and three (9.7%) of the four hourly group felt either uncomfortable or very uncomfortable caring for their child at home. There was no statistical difference between the two groups (P = 0.71, Fischer's exact two-tailed test).", "Parents were asked their degree of satisfaction with their child's treatment in hospital, ranging from very unsatisfied to very satisfied. Only one set of parents in each group felt dissatisfied with their child's hospitalization.", "Parents were also asked to comment on how long it took for their child to get back to his/her 'normal self' (i.e. back to kindergarten, school, or back on their usual prophylactic medications). The mean (Ϯ SD) time was 3.9 (Ϯ 3.1) days in the three hourly group, vs 3.7 (Ϯ 2.2) days in the four hourly group (P = 0.859). The use of additional salbutamol after discharge was for a mean (Ϯ SD) of 5.5 (Ϯ 3.3) days in the three hourly group, and 6.0 (Ϯ 3.7) days in the four hourly group (P = 0.556).", "The mean (Ϯ SD) length of hospital stay was 48.94 (Ϯ 20.61) hours in the three hourly group, and 54.88 (Ϯ 32.59) hours in the four hourly group. This mean difference of 5.94 h was not statistically significant (P = 0.672).", "The aim of our study was to determine whether discharge from hospital on three hourly salbutamol was as safe and effective as discharge on four hourly salbutamol. There is limited literature addressing the optimal timing of discharge of asthmatics from hospital. The only published study, in adults, 7 found that most patients were discharged from hospital earlier than the criteria set by the British Thoracic Society. 8, 9 However, in that study there were no adverse outcomes, suggesting that current criteria could be liberalized to the earlier discharge of adult patients. 7 We could find no published studies to determine whether this is also true in children.", "Several key findings emerged from this study. Children discharged earlier (on three hourly salbutamol) had no increase in either hospital readmission or re-presentation to the EDt. In fact, in both groups in this study there was no hospital readmission nor re-presentation to the ED within 7 days of discharge. Given that our sample size was calculated on an assumption of a 10% representation rate, and given that there were no re-presentations in the three hourly group, we terminated the study early as it was clear that an extremely large sample would be necessary to show any statistical difference between the two regimens.", "The rate of re-presentation to local doctors was similar between the two groups, 71.8% in the three hourly and 74.1% in the four hourly group. The majority of patients attended not because they were sick but because the hospital doctors had recommended it. This was more common in the three hourly (86%) than the four hourly (69%) group, which could reflect more cautious advice given by the attending team when sending children home earlier than usual.", "Parents' perceptions of the timing of their child's discharge, either 'too early' or 'too late', is an important measure of the optimum time of discharge. Similar numbers of parents in the three hourly (15.6%) and four hourly (16.1%) groups felt their child was discharged 'too early'. While 9.7% of the four hourly group felt they were sent home 'later than necessary', none of the three hourly group had similar concerns.", "Other measures compared included how comfortable the parents were caring for their child at home immediately after discharge; how satisfied they were with their child's treatment; the number of days until their child was 'back to normal'; and the number of days of salbutamol usage after discharge. Again, the differences between the three and four hourly groups were small and not statistically significant.", "Although the 6 h difference in length of stay was not statistically significant, it is potentially a clinically important difference in view of the large number of asthma admissions each year (nearly 2000 at RAHC in 1996). Assuming the same standard deviations in length of stay, a sample size of approximately 500 patients would demonstrate that a 6 h difference in length of stay was statistically significant.", "Our study had several limitations. The sample size was small and would need to be substantially larger to demonstrate a statistically significant reduction in hospital length of stay. The study was not blinded. Parents, the house staff and the attending paediatrician were aware of the group to which their child was allocated. However, the principal investigator, who performed all follow-up telephone questionnaires, was blinded as to the allocation of the child. Because of logistical difficulties in follow-up we excluded parents who were non-English speaking and those who did not have a telephone, characteristics which could potentially make re-presentation more likely. This limits the generalizability of our findings to English speaking parents with telephones.", "We have demonstrated little difference in safety and effectiveness between discharging children on three or four hourly nebulized salbutamol. Additional benefits include less disruption to family life and a more calming, familiar environment for the child to recuperate in. There is also less exposure to hospital acquired infections. Possible benefits for the hospital are increased bed availability and lower costs. Potential risks of earlier discharge are increased parental concern and anxiety, the need for further medical review by the local doctor or the ED, or even hospital readmission. However, none of these risks were evident in our study.", "In view of the comparable outcome between three hourly and four hourly discharge, it may be preferable to alter practice and switch patients as they improve from second hourly to four hourly nebulizers and discharge them at that point. Thus, all patients would be discharged on four hourly nebulizers, and none on three hourly nebulizers. This would overcome the possibility that a regime of three hourly nebulizing is excessive for the parents. Other steps to ensure the success of earlier discharge should include thorough asthma education, provision of a written asthma management plan, liaison with the family doctor, and arrangement of appropriate follow-up."], "domain_risk_levels": ["Low Risk", "Some Concerns", "Low Risk", "Low Risk", "Some Concerns"], "overall_risk_level": "Some Concerns", "paper_id": "29336", "pdf_hash": "e9ca4b17fd2255e82f36a63277d4b2fd2c2f5246", "result": [{"addedParagraphs": [], "ctxP1Score": "0.496", "ctxP2Score": "0.271", "ctxP3Score": "0.265", "ctxParagraph1": "Children were randomized within 24 h of admission, after parents had given informed consent for them to enter the study. The two treatment arms were discharge on three hourly and discharge on four hourly nebulized salbutamol. Randomization was achieved using sealed, shuffled opaque envelopes containing prerandomized allocation, blocked into equal 1:1 proportions for each medical team. Patients were randomized by the attending team's resident or registrar, by selecting an envelope from the shuffled pile. The parents and the attending house staff and paediatrician were aware of the group to which their child was randomized. However, the principal investigator (MS), who performed the followup questionnaire, was blinded as to which group the patient belonged.", "ctxParagraph1Feedback": null, "ctxParagraph2": "We conducted a randomized controlled parallel group study. All children over 18 months of age admitted to the hospital with acute asthma were eligible to enter the study. Patients were excluded if they had non-English speaking parents, no telephone, or chronic cardiac or neurological disease.", "ctxParagraph2Feedback": null, "ctxParagraph3": "Following discharge, all parents were interviewed one to two weeks later via a telephone administered questionnaire by MS (Appendix 1). The questionnaire had been previously piloted in 10 sets of parents to ensure no ambiguity of questions.", "ctxParagraph3Feedback": null, "domain_number": 1, "expertExplanation": "only information about randomization methods is a statement that the study is randomized.", "expertPrediction": "no information", "gptExplanation": "The children were randomiz