[{"annotator": "", "document": ["There have been few studies of GH dose responses in Turner's syndrome. We have therefore compared the growth effect of two doses of subcutaneous rGH: 0 . 45 (D1) or 0 . 90 (D2) IU/kg/week. DESIGN Multicentre study with two parallel randomized groups treated with D1 or D2 dose for one year, then with D2 for the second and third years in both groups. PATIENTS Ninety-seven girls with Turner's syndrome aged from 4 . 8 to 16 . 5 years. RESULTS The first mean height velocity (HV) was significantly higher with D2. At one year the girls changed from D1 to D2 showed a further acceleration in HV. During second and third years HV remained above the mean for untreated Turner girls, in both groups. Mean cumulative height gains over the 3 years were 1 . 06 and 1 . 17 SDS/CA (Ranke's Turner standard) in groups G1 and G2 respectively. Bone maturation, over 36 months, was 33 . 7 (G1) and 31 . 9 (G2) months. CONCLUSION These results suggest that, if a higher initial GH dose is associated with a greater net initial height gain, the duration of treatment might affect the long-term results. Intermittent treatment should be considered.", "Among the large number of therapeutic clinical trials for the improvement of growth in Turner's syndrome, many have compared the effects of GH alone to those of GH together with steroids (Vanderschueren-Lodeweyckx et al., 1990; Job et al., 1991; Stahnke et al., 1992; Rosenfeld et al., 1992) . Acceleration of height velocity was observed although no definite conclusion could be drawn on the degree of final height improvement (Rosenfeld et al., 1992; Rochiccioli et al., 1994) . Excessive skeletal maturation with oestrogen (Vanderschueren-Lodeweyckx et al., 1990) and other side-effects such as moderate virilization with oxandrolone (Stahnke et al., 1992; Job, 1993) were reported, both dependent upon the dose. When recombinant GH was tested alone, few trials documented the dose-response relation between GH and growth (Takano et al., 1992; Spoudeas et al., 1993) . The multicentre study reported here was designed to compare the effects of two randomly allocated GH doses (D1 0 : 45, D2 0 : 90 IU/kg/week) for one year in 97 girls with Turner's syndrome. After the first year of treatment, all patients treated with the lower dose were changed to 0 . 90 IU/kg/week for the 2 subsequent years.", "Inclusion criteria were: karyotype confirming Turner's syndrome; height of 1 . 5 standard deviation (SD) or more below the mean for chronological age (CA) according to Sempe ´standards for French girls (Sempe ´, 1979) ; height velocity (HV) below the mean for bone age (BA) according to the same standards; BA estimated by referring physicians below 12 years and below CA, according to the atlas of Greulich and Pyle (1959) ; weight between ÿ2 and 3 SD of weight for height. Birth length and weight as well as parental heights were documented. Laboratory data included were: plasma GH levels after two stimulation tests above 7 . 5 g/l (or 10 g/l if only 1 test was performed), fasting blood glucose and free T4 within their normal ranges.", "Exclusion criteria were: X0/XY Turner syndrome if not gonadectomized, patients with altered renal or cardiac function, previous neoplastic or other severe chronic illness, previous GH or thyroid hormone treatment. Androgen or oestrogen therapy had to be discontinued for at least one year and/or not planned for the next year.", "The protocol was approved by the Comite ´d'Ethique des Ho ˆpitaux de Paris, and informed written consents were obtained from all patients. Patients were stratified according to BA (7 . 5 years or >7 : 5 years) and randomly allocated for one year to one of two GH-dose groups: group G1 was treated with 0 . 45 IU/kg/week (D1) and group G2 with 0 . 90 IU/kg/week (D2). The D2 dose was given to every patient for the second and third years. G1 and G2 patients remained in these groups during the second and third years of treatment and were analysed separately and compared with each other. Ethinyloestradiol (<25 g/day) was then permitted for girls with BA > 12 years. GH (Maxomat, Sanofi-Winthrop, France) was given subcutaneously in the evening on 6 days per week.", "Laboratory tests were performed at the start and every 3 months (first year) or 6 months (second and third year). Anti-GH and anti-Escherichia coli protein (ECP) antibodies were centrally assayed, as previously described (Chatelain et al., 1994) as well as IGF-I, the latter during only the first year (Chatelain et al., 1983) . BA was centrally rated (Greulich & Pyle, 1959) by two independent paediatric radiologists.", "For height, HV and weight, the SD scores (SDS) were defined as: SDS x ÿ X =SD where x is the observed value of each parameter, X the mean value and SD the standard deviation of this parameter in the reference population. SDs were obtained from the reference tables of either French girls (Sempe ´et al., 1979) or Ranke Turner's girls (Ranke et al., 1983) standards. An acceleration of 2 cm/year or more was required to qualify a patient as 'responding well' to GH treatment. Comparability of patients' main characteristics at inclusion between dose groups was checked using Student's or Fisher's exact tests. Growth and biological parameters of the two groups were compared by twotailed tests (Student) with an P-level of 0 . 05. Results were expressed as mean 6 SEM.", "Ninety-seven girls aged from 4 . 8 to 16 . 5 were included from 19 paediatric endocrinology clinics. The parameters of the patients are given in Table 1 . No significant difference was found between the two groups for any of these parameters.", "Nine patients discontinued GH treatment over the 3 years: 4 G1 patients, due either to poor compliance with study visits (n 1) or to inefficacy of treatment (n 2) or as required by protocol amendment (BA > 12 and height gain 42cm for the last 6 months) in 1 patient; in 5 G2 patients, discontinuation was related either to family choice (n 3) or to adverse events (n 2). Compliance with treatment was usually good. The proportion of missed injections was above 10% in 20 patients (11 G1 and 9 G2), usually for a single 3-month period over the 3 years of treatment, repeatedly in only 4 (2 of each group).", "Mean growth parameters and bone maturation calculated for all treated patients are shown in Table 2 .", "Significant differences (P 0 : 0001) in mean height velocity (HV) between the two dose groups were observed only for the first year: 5 . 5 cm/year (or 1 . 4 SDS/BA) in G1 vs 6 . 7 cm/year (or 2 . 5 SDS/BA) in G2. Mean HV was markedly accelerated in both groups at month 6 (Fig. 1 ) and during the first year. Doubling the GH dose (from 0 . 45 to 0 . 9 IU/kg/w) at month 12, in G1 patients, significantly increased HV calculated between months 12 and 18 compared to 6th and 12th month (from 1 . 1 . 9 SD/BA; P 0 : 02). Although progressive attenuation of the effect with time was observed, HV remained above the mean for untreated Turner patients (Ranke) during the 3 years of treatment in both groups.", "Individual HV before treatment and over the 3 successive years are plotted in Fig. 2 . Responders to treatment were 21 out of 47 G1 patients (45%) vs 35 out of 50 G2 patients (70%), a significantly (P 0 : 014) higher proportion. Calculation (on the basis of 2 SDS/BA, Ranke) gave similar results (43 . 5% in G1 vs 68% in G2, P 0 : 023).", "A significant difference between G1 and G2 groups was observed in mean height gain (SDS/CA, Ranke) at 1 year (0 : 30 6 0 : 03 in 46 G1 vs 0 : 54 6 0 : 02 in 49 G2, P < 0 : 0001) and at 2 years (0 : 73 6 0 : 05 in 45 G1 vs 0 : 91 6 0 : 04 in 46 G2, P 0 : 0061). At 3 years, mean height gain was 1 : 06 6 0 : 06 in G1 (n 42) and 1 : 17 6 0 : 05 in G2 group (n 45) but the difference was no longer significant. The height gain results were not modified by excluding the girls (5 G1 and 11 G2) who received oestrogens during the third year: 1 : 03 6 0 : 07 and 1 : 20 6 0 : 06 SDS/CA in G1 (n 37) and G2 (n 34) groups respectively. The 3-year height gain was well predicted by the first 6-month growth velocity response: 1 . 21 and 1 . 22 SDS/CA (Ranke) in the 18 G1 and 31 G2 'responder' patients, a significantly higher gain than in 'non-responder' patients (0 . 93 and 1 . 07 SDS/CA G1 and G2 groups respectively) (P 0 : 06).", "Bone maturation did not differ significantly at any time between the two groups over the 36 months, 33 . 7 in G1 compared to 31 . 9 months in G2 group. Weight was stable in the G2 group (1 . 6 SD) and increased significantly from 1 . 15 to 1 . 43 SDS in G1 group (although groups never differed significantly). Mean plasma IGF-I (IU/ml) increased in both groups for the first 3 months: from 1 . 02 to 1 . 22 in G1 group and from 1 . 00 to 1 . 55 in G2 group. Over the first year, the increment was significantly higher in G2 group (IGF-I values at M12: 1 . 48 vs 1 . 21 IU/ml in G1).", "Additional analyses show that, in both groups, the younger the initial bone age, the higher the height gain expressed in SDS/CA at 1 year. They also show that this height gain was significantly lower in patients with karyotype 45 X0 than in other karyotypes, whatever the group: 0 . 00 vs 0 . 19 SD in G1 group and 0 . 36 vs 0 . 53 SD in G2 group. However, this was no longer true if one considered the 3-year height gain.", "Sixty-four out of 97 patients reported 178 adverse events (AEs) during the 281 treatment-years of this study. The more frequent AEs were, according to the WHO classification: 'applicationsite disorders' (n 24, local pain or reaction to injection), 'resistance mechanism disorders' (48 AEs: 15 otitis media, 14 pharyngitis, 13 upper respiratory tract infections), 'general disorders' (23 AEs: 8 influenza-like symptoms and 2 fevers), 'gastro-intestinal disorders' (17 AEs), 'skin and appendage disorders' (14 AEs, 1 naevus variation). Twenty-eight hospitalizations for surgery (8 ENT and 8 plastic surgery operations), seemingly unrelated to GH therapy, were classified as severe adverse events. In fact, two severe adverse events were observed in G2 patients. In one case, one pemphigoid bullosa was treated and healed with steroids; GH was considered unlikely to be responsible but was transiently discontinued due to the use of steroids. In the other case, an acute episode of severe headache with fainting was observed after 10 months of GH treatment. Fundoscopic examination was normal. Brain CT scan and MRI confirmed a 1-cm image in the grey nuclei area, known before the onset of GH treatment; ventricles were normal. A pseudo-tumour cerebri episode was considered. The patient remains otherwise asymptomatic and is doing well.", "Mean plasma fasting glucose and glycosylated haemoglobin (HbA1C) remained stable. Mean free T4 decreased slightly, but not significantly, over the 3 years without clinical effects: from 18 . 6 and 18 . 4 to 16 . 6 and 16 . 1 pmol/l, in G1 and G2 respectively. Significant variations were observed in both groups over the first year in the mean values for plasma sodium (decreased) and phosphate (increased). Significant variations in concentrations were limited to the G2 group for bilirubin (from 8 : 2 6 0 : 5 to 9 : 3 6 0 : 8; maximum individual value, 28 mol/l) and alkaline phosphatase (increased values at 6th month).", "Anti-GH antibodies were present before GH treatment in 22 out of 97 patients with a maximal binding capacity (MBC) of 2 . 14%. During treatment, 11 out of 47 G1 and 8 out of 35 G2 girls had a MBC 45 . 11 and 44 . 68% in G1 and G2, respectively. Subsequent evaluations showed a decrease in MBC in all patients regardless of GH continuation. For anti-ECP antibodies (always 0% at inclusion) mean MBC was 0 : 4 6 0 : 1 (range 0 . 0-1 . 9) in G1 and 0 : 4 6 0 : 1 (range 0 . 0-1 . 9) in G2 at month 36.", "GH therapy increases height velocity in Turner's syndrome as documented by several comparative studies, including trials with untreated control groups (Rosenfeld et al., 1992; Stephure et al., 1993) . However, information remains limited regarding the effect on height velocity of dose and frequency of GH administration. GH doses tested usually varied from 0 . 5 to 1 . 2 IU/kg/week but only three trials compared the effects of several GH doses on height velocity (Takano et al., 1992; Stahnke et al., 1992; Spoudeas et al., 1993) .", "The results of our two GH doses show that using, for the first year, 0 . 9 IU/kg/w results in a mean growth acceleration (from 3 . 79 to 6 . 73 cm/year) significantly higher than that observed with 0 . 45 IU (from 3 . 95 to 5 . 47 cm/year). In previously published studies, growth acceleration observed at one year was 1 . 7 times greater with 1 . 0 than with 0 . 5 IU/kg/w (Takano et al., 1992) or 1 . 3 times greater with 30 than with 20 IU/m 2 /w (Spoudeas et al., 1993) . In the light of these observations, the apparent cost-benefit ratio at one year could favour the highest GH dose. Furthermore, during the second year of our study, G1 patients, when treated with 0 . 9 IU/kg/week (D2 dose), did not reach the height velocity of G2 patients in the first year (Table 2 ). However, over 3 years, the G1 patients who received 83 . 4% of the G2 GH dose grew only 0 . 5 cm less (mean) than G2 patients. At a mean weight of 25 kg (as for our patients at inclusion) treatment with the D1 dose for 1 out of 3 years provided a saving of around 580 IU GH per patient.", "This points to the complexity of the dose-response relation in GH treated patients with Turner's syndrome. Our data suggest that the duration of GH treatment alters the apparent benefit of the high initial GH dose; therefore, the choice of the GH dose to be administered for the first year must also take into account the expected duration of treatment. Whether additional treatment, such as extremely low dose oestrogen, should be added, and when, remains an unsolved issue. Cost-benefit considerations call for additional studies, including some with intermittent treatments.", "Variations in response to treatment are observed among individuals regardless of the dose (Fig. 2 ). The low titre-low affinity of the rare anti-GH antibodies detected is unlikely to result in impairment in growth response. When age at the start of treatment is considered (with similar GH dosage), a greater acceleration is observed in Takano's study and in ours compared to that of Spoudeas; however, the mean age of Takano's patients and ours is greater. This seems contradictory to our observation that the height gain for 1 year is greater when bone age is less. Such variations might result from individual sensitivity to GH treatment suggesting that GH dose should be adapted individually. The observation (Chaussain et al., 1993) that increasing GH dose from 1 . 4 to 2 . 1 IU/kg/week in 'nonresponding' Turner's patients could still increase height velocity supports the notion of individual tailoring of GH dose.", "The question arises whether 0 . 9 IU/kg/week can be considered as a supraphysiological or pharmacological GH dose. It is known that GH physiological secretion varies dramatically from birth to adulthood (Albertson-Wikland et al., 1989; Miller et al., 1993) , being higher post-natally and at the time of puberty. In this study, mean plasma IGF-I levels observed during treatment with the D2 dose (0 . 9 IU/kg/week), although significantly higher than with D1, are not abnormally high for sex and age. Should a supraphysiological GH dose be used, one would expect abnormally high IGF-I levels.", "The significance of bone maturation in Turner's patients should be regarded with caution, since this condition may be considered to have similarities to the skeletal dysplasia. Bearing this restriction in mind, no difference in bone maturation related to GH dose was observed during the 3 years of this study. Although this should be interpreted with care (since 'bone age' rating referred to atlas and methods developed in normal girls), the improvement of mean height observed at 3 years (1 . 2 SDS according to Turner Ranke standards) seems encouraging if one considers the objective of final height improvement (Rochiccioli et al., 1994) .", "The choice of the GH dose regimen in the treatment of Turner's syndrome remains difficult. This study shows that the first 6-month growth response is a predictor of the total height gain at 3 years and shows that a higher GH dose results in a greater net initial height gain but does not prevent the waning effect. Whether other treatment algorithms would diminish such a waning effect remains to be established. In any case, the efficacy of a GH-treatment regimen in Turner's syndrome should be evaluated on a long-term basis."], "domain_risk_levels": ["High Risk", "Some Concerns", "High Risk", "Low Risk", "Some Concerns"], "overall_risk_level": "High Risk", "paper_id": "29356", "pdf_hash": "60c64e073717b90aaf46eaa69b78e3d80873a293", "result": [{"addedParagraphs": [], "ctxP1Score": "0.171", "ctxP2Score": "0.168", "ctxP3Score": "0.161", "ctxParagraph1": "Individual HV before treatment and over the 3 successive years are plotted in Fig. 2 . Responders to treatment were 21 out of 47 G1 patients (45%) vs 35 out of 50 G2 patients (70%), a significantly (P 0 : 014) higher proportion. Calculation (on the basis of 2 SDS/BA, Ranke) gave similar results (43 . 5% in G1 vs 68% in G2, P 0 : 023).", "ctxParagraph1Feedback": null, "ctxParagraph2": "For height, HV and weight, the SD scores (SDS) were defined as: SDS x ÿ X =SD where x is the observed value of each parameter, X the mean value and SD the standard deviation of this parameter in the reference population. SDs were obtained from the reference tables of either French girls (Sempe ´et al., 1979) or Ranke Turner's girls (Ranke et al., 1983) standards. An acceleration of 2 cm/year or more was required to qualify a patient as 'responding well' to GH treatment. Comparability of patients' main characteristics at inclusion between dose groups was checked using Student's or Fisher's exact tests. Growth and biological parameters of the two groups were compared by twotailed tests (Student) with an P-level of 0 . 05. Results were expressed as mean 6 SEM.", "ctxParagraph2Feedback": null, "ctxParagraph3": "The results of our two GH doses show that using, for the first year, 0 . 9 IU/kg/w results in a mean growth acceleration (from 3 . 79 to 6 . 73 cm/year) significantly higher than that observed with 0 . 45 IU (from 3 . 95 to 5 . 47 cm/year). In previously published studies, growth acceleration observed at one year was 1 . 7 times greater with 1 . 0 than with 0 . 5 IU/kg/w (Takano et al., 1992) or 1 . 3 times greater with 30 than with 20 IU/m 2 /w (Spoudeas et al., 1993) . In the light of these observations, the apparent cost-benefit ratio at one year could favour the highest GH dose. Furthermore, during the second year of our study, G1 patients, when treated with 0 . 9 IU/kg/week (D2 dose), did not reach the height velocity of G2 patients in the first year (Table 2 ). However, over 3 years, the G1 patients who received 83 . 4% of the G2 GH dose grew only 0 . 5 cm less (mean) than G2 patients. At a mean weight of 25 kg (as for our patients at inclusion) treatment with the D1 dose for 1 out of 3 years provided a saving of around 580 IU GH per patient.", "ctxParagraph3Feedback": null, "domain_number": 1, "expertExplanation": "only information about randomization methods is a statement that the study is randomized", "expertPrediction": "no information", "gptExplanation": "The text does not provide information about the method used to generate the allocation sequence. Therefore, it is not possible to determine if the allocation sequence was random or not.", "gptPrediction": "no", "gptResponse": "{
