T and 8 plastic surgery operations), seemingly unrelated to GH therapy, were classified as severe adverse events. In fact, two severe adverse events were observed in G2 patients. In one case, one pemphigoid bullosa was treated and healed with steroids; GH was considered unlikely to be responsible but was transiently discontinued due to the use of steroids. In the other case, an acute episode of severe headache with fainting was observed after 10 months of GH treatment. Fundoscopic examination was normal. Brain CT scan and MRI confirmed a 1-cm image in the grey nuclei area, known before the onset of GH treatment; ventricles were normal. A pseudo-tumour cerebri episode was considered. The patient remains otherwise asymptomatic and is doing well.", "ctxParagraph3Feedback": null, "domain_number": 4, "expertExplanation": "", "expertPrediction": "qa-na", "gptExplanation": "Knowledge of the intervention received (GH treatment) could have influenced the assessment of the outcome, as it may have introduced bias in the interpretation of the results. There may have been expectations or preconceived notions about the effectiveness of GH treatment that could have influenced the evaluation of patients' response to the treatment.", "gptPrediction": "yes", "gptResponse": "{
