[{"annotator": "", "document": ["The objective of this study was to compare the efficacy and safety of triamcinolone acetonide hydrofluoroaikane 134a (HFA) (chlorofluorocarbon [CFC] free) with those of triamcinolone acetonide P-12 (CFC containing) in children with chronic asthma. This multicenter, randomized, double-blind, parallel-group study comprised 473 children (6 to 13 years of age) with asthma. Patients received 12 weeks of treatment with 150, 300, or 600 lag of triamcinolone acetonide HFA daily given as 1, 2, or 4 puffs (75 pg/puff) or the same doses of triamcinolone acetonide P-12 after a 3-to 28-day baseline period. Mean percent change in forced expiratory volume in 1 second was 12.17% to 22.02% with HFA and 13.53% to 22.62% with P-12. A1buterol use decreased by 2, 2.7, and 3.6 puffs/d with the HFA 150-, 300-, and 600-pg groups, respectively, and by 2.2, 2.4, and 3 puffs/d for the respective P-12 groups. Significant improvement (P < 0.05) in symptoms was noted with both formulations. Overall, both formulations were similarly effective in improving pulmonary function and relieving asthma symptoms, and in general, both formulations were more effective at the higher doses of triamcinolone acetonide. Both formulations were well tolerated. Based on these results, we concluded that triamcinolone acetonide HFA inhalation aerosol is safe and effective in children with asthma and produces effects on pulmonary function and asthma symptoms similar to those produced by triamcinolone acetonide P-12.", "Asthma affects an estimated 10 to 25 million people in the United States and accounts for 1% of health care costs in the United States. 1'2 Children CURRENT THERAPEUTIC RESEARCH @ are disproportionately affected, representing up to 10% of the population in western countries, as opposed to 5% of the adult population. 8 However, the widespread use of inhaled corticosteroids may be contributing to declining hospital admission rates and lengths of stay related to childhood asthma. 4 The efficacy and safety of the corticosteroid triamcinolone acetonide for the treatment of chronic asthma in adults and children 6 years of age and older have been well documented. 5-s Triamcinolone acetonide has been marketed since 1984 in the United States as a pressurized, oral, metered-dose inhaler (MDI) with the chlorofluorocarbon (CFC) propellant P-12* (dichlorodifluoromethane). However, because CFCs are now known to deplete the stratospheric ozone layer, the United States and other nations have agreed to phase out their use. 9'1~ The non-CFC propellant hydrofluoroalkane 134a (HFA; 1,1,1,2-tetrafluoroethane) is a safe alternative to CFC propellants for use in MDIs and has little potential for ozone depletion. 11 '12 In compliance with the international requirement for environmentally safe products, the triamcinolone acetonide inhalation aerosol has been reformulated using HFA as the propellant.", "The present study was designed to evaluate the efficacy and safety of triamcinolone acetonide HFA at total daily doses of 150, 300, and 600 ~g (1, 2, or 4 puffs twice daily) in children with chronic asthma. The doserelated effects of this new formulation were compared with those of the currently marketed triamcinolone acetonide inhalation aerosol at the same doses.", "Boys and premenarchal girls 6 to 13 years of age who had at least a 1-year history of perennial asthma requiring daily asthma medication and inhaled beta-agonist use for at least the previous month were eligible to participate in the study. Patients with life-threatening asthma, anoxic seizures, significant hypercapnia, or recent hospitalization for asthma were excluded from the study. Systemic corticosteroid use (once within the previous month or more than 2 courses during the previous year) or any significant clinical/laboratory abnormalities or clinical conditions were also reason for exclusion. The study protocol and informed consent were reviewed and approved by independent institutional review boards. Written informed consent was obtained from each parent or legal guardian. ", "During the treatment period, patients were randomly allocated to i of 6 treatment groups: triamcinolone acetonide P-12 (75 ~Lg/puff) at a dose of 150, 300, or 600 ~g/d (1, 2, or 4 puffs twice daily) or triamcinolone acetonide HFA (75 ~g/puff) at a dose of 150, 300, or 600 ~tg/d (1, 2, or 4 puffs twice daily). A built-in spacer-mouthpiece was used for both the HFA and P-12 formulations to ensure consistent delivery of 75 ~g of triamcinolone acetonide.", "The study drugs were supplied in identical canisters to guarantee a double-blind environment. The first dose of study medication was administered under supervision to ensure proper technique. Asthma medications other than the study drug and inhaled albuterol ("as needed") were prohibited during the study. Patients were permitted to receive intranasal glucocorticoids, oral decongestants, intranasal cromolyn sodium, oral antibiotics, or oral antihistamines for treatment of allergies or infections.", "This double-blind, randomized, parallel-group study was conducted at 43 centers in the United States. The study consisted of a screening visit, a 3-to 28-day baseline period, and a 12-week treatment period. At the initial screening visit, pulmonary function, including forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV 1) and forced midexpiratory flow rate (FEF25%_75%), were assessed after a 6-hour albuterol-free period. Patients were eligible ifFEV 1 was between 50% and 100% of predicted or personal best values. Patients who fulfilled initial eligibility criteria entered into the baseline period, during which they continued their usual asthma medications. During baseline, patients were instructed in the use of a portable meter to measure morning (AM) and evening (PM) peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR), and they were given an albuterol MDI for use on an as-needed basis. Diary cards were dispensed to patients to record PEFR, daily use of albuterol and other drugs, asthma symptom ratings, and number of nighttime awakenings caused by asthma and adverse events.", "To participate in the randomization phase, patients were required to use a total of -> 15 inhalations of albuterol on 3 of the 4 previous days but no more than 12 puffs in any single day. In addition, patients needed to demonstrate ->1 of the following on any 1 of the 3 days: a total daily symptom score (morning + evening) of ->3 but -<6; AM]PM PEFR variability of ->20%; -> 1 nighttime awakening because of asthma symptoms; or FEV1 15% lower than the screening value. FEV 1 must have been 50% to -80% of predicted or personal best value with an increase ->15% after 2 puffs of albuterol. If the patient was not sufficiently symptomatic to enter the study after 1 week, asthma medication doses could be reduced by -50%, followed by a second 50% reduction after the second week, if necessary.", "Patients not meeting the symptom criteria after the second reduction could not be included in the randomization phase.", "During the 12-week treatment phase, patients returned to the clinic at weeks 1 and 2 and every 14 days thereafter. At each visit, the following procedures were performed: examination of the mouth and throat for fungal infection, collection of old and distribution of new study drug (and albuterol, if required), pulmonary function testing, dispensing of new diary cards, and review of dosing technique. Daily diary recordings and PEFR assessments were continued throughout the 12-week treatment phase. Patients unable to maintain adequate control of asthma symptoms and those requiring >-12 inhalations of albuterol in 1 day were discontinued under the classification "test drug ineffective."", "The primary efficacy variables were mean percent change in FEV1 from baseline to end point and mean change in daily albuterol use. Secondary variables included mean percent change in FEF25%_75 % from baseline to end point, as well as changes in AM and PM PEFR, nighttime awakenings, patient efficacy ratings, and asthma symptom scores. Asthma symptoms were rated on a 7-point scale, ranging from 0 (no symptoms) to 6 (incapacitating symptoms requiring physician intervention). Baseline for diary card variables was the mean measurement for the 4 days (albuterol use) or 5 days (PEFR, symptom scores, nighttime awakenings) before starting study treatment. The end point for all diary card variables was the mean score of the data from the last 7 treatment days.", "Analyses were also performed on the 24-and 48-hour AM PEFR values and at 48 hours for daily albuterol use.", "Adverse events were recorded on the daily diary card. At each visit, the card was reviewed by study personnel; patients were interviewed for details regarding these events; and an assessment was made of severity and relationship to study drug. Physical examination results and vital sign indices were also evaluated, as well as various laboratory values (serum chemistry, liver function, hematology, and urinalysis).", "The primary analysis compared the mean change (or mean percent change) from baseline to end point using a 1-way analysis of variance to determine the treatment effect on the outcome measures within each treatment formulation. Pairwise comparisons were also performed within each formulation. Within-formulation analyses were based on an F test with a 5% level of significance.", "Across-formulation analyses were performed only on the assessable population (patients who took ->70% of their prescribed study medication doses). These analyses consisted of the following: comparison of therapeutic equivalence between formulations at the same dose levels using 95% and 90% confidence intervals; across dose levels, first determining that the overall model, including both formulations, could detect a treatment effect, then determining whether the effect is secondary to the different formulation; and regression line analysis.", "Four hundred seventy-three patients were enrolled in the study and included in the intent-to-treat population. The 6 treatment groups were well matched for demographics, baseline FEVz, and albuterol use at randomization (Table I ). Of the 473 enrolled patients, 374 (79.1%) completed all 12 weeks of treatment. The percentage of patients who withdrew because of treatment ineffectiveness was similar in all groups (12% to 18%), except for a lower incidence in the HFA 600-1~g group (8%). ", "The 3 methods used to compare the HFA and P-12 formulations produced results supporting therapeutic equivalence. Comparisons between HFA and P-12 formulations within dose levels showed that the 2 formulations were therapeutically equivalent at all 3 doses for albuterol use, AM PEFR, and nighttime awakenings. Although differences in FEV1 and 24hour symptom scores were evident between formulations, they were not significant.", "With regard to comparisons across dose levels, no significant differences between formulations were noted for albuterol use, 24-hour symptom scores, or nighttime awakenings. Linear regression analysis showed an overlap between the 95% confidence bands for albuterol use, AM PEFR, and nighttime awakenings across all dose levels for both treatments, which confirms the similarity between formulations. Overlap across the dose range was incomplete for FEV 1 and asthma symptom scores, with the HFA formulation performing better at some dose levels.", "Significant improvements in FEV1 were observed with all doses of both formulations. The mean percent change in FEV 1 at the end of the treatment period ranged from 12.17% to 22.02% for the HFA groups (overall treatment, P = 0.055), and from 13.53% to 22.62% for the P-12 groups (overall treatment, P = 0.061) (Table II ). TAA HFA = triamcinolone acetonide inhalation aerosol using hydrofluoroalkane 134a as the propellant; TAA P-12 = TAA inhalation aerosol using P-12 as the propellant. * % Change = mean % improvement from baseline.", "Results of the analyses of the secondary pulmonary function variables are presented in Table III . Both formulations produced improvements from baseline in AM and PM PEFR and FEF25%_75 %.", "In the 24-and 48-hour analyses, the HFA 150-~g and 600-~g groups showed significant improvements in AM PEFR at 24 and 48 hours after dosing compared with baseline. The HFA 300-~tg group did not exhibit any significant difference between predose and postdose fmdings. For the P-12 formulation, the 600-~g group showed significant improvements in AM TAA-HFA = triamcinolone acetonide inhalation aerosol using hydrofluoroalkane 134a as the propellant; TAA P-12 = TAA inhalation aerosol using P-12 as the propellant.", "PEFR at 24 and 48 hours. The 300-tLg group showed a significant difference at 48 hours only, and the 150-~g group did not show a significant difference at either time point. None of the treatment groups showed any significant improvements in albuterol use at 48 hours.", "Albuterol use decreased by 2, 2.7, and 3.6 puffs/d for the HFA 150-, 300-, and 600-~g groups, respectively, and by 2.2, 2.4, and 3 puffs/d for the respective P-12 groups (figure). The overall treatment effect was siguifi- TAA P-12 = triamcinolone acetonide inhalation aerosol using P-12 as the propellant; TAA HFA = TAA inhalation aerosol using hydrofluoroalkane 134a as the propellant.", "No serious laboratory abnormalities were observed during the study, and no patient discontinued treatment because of laboratory abnormalities. An increasing shift was noted in several patients for neutrophil and eosinophil counts, although the findings were consistent with asthma and no dose-related trends were observed.", "Although statistically significant increases in pulmonary function measurements were demonstrated from baseline to end point for all doses, comparisons between doses were only marginally significant or not significant for these variables. Because ethical considerations prohibited using a placebo group in this study, efficacy assessments were based only on comparisons of 3 doses of study medication. It is likely that the between-dose differences in the pulmonary function measurements lacked statistical significance because patients in all dose groups demonstrated such substantial improvement that pulmonary function was nearly normalized.", "In general, higher doses of triamcinolone acetonide produced increased symptom improvement. For many variables, patients in the 600-~g groups showed statistically significantly greater improvement compared with those in the 150-~g groups. Not unexpectedly, significant differences between adjacent groups occurred less often. This lack of significant difference between adjacent dose groups of individual inhaled corticosteroids is well recognized. In a review of 10 dose-response studies of beclomethasone dipropionate, budesonide, and fluticasone by Pedersen and O'Byrne, 13 9 studies failed to show statistically significant differences in clinical effects between adjacent dose groups. The authors stated that al-though at least 4-fold differences in dose are normally required to detect significant differences in effect, even these large variations in dose do not always elicit significantly different responses.", "For albuterol use, AM PEFR, and nighttime awakenings, the 2 formulations were therapeutically equivalent at all 3 dose levels. Although therapeutic equivalence could not be demonstrated for FEV1 and 24-hour asthma symptom scores, the triamcinolone acetonide HFA formulation performed better than the triamcinolone acetonide P-12 product at some dose levels. The lack of treatment effect with regard to FEV1 and FEF25%_75 % is likely caused by the large, but similar, improvements observed in all 6 patient groups.", "A small segment of the study population did not respond to the assigned treatment. Eight percent of patients in the triamcinolone acetonide HFA 600-~g group and 12% to 18% of patients in the other 5 groups were withdrawn because of inefficacy of the study drug. Other than in the triamcinolone acetonide HFA 600-~g group, there was no apparent dose relationship to withdrawals due to inefficacy. Dose responsiveness to inhaled corticosteroids has been difficult to show between comparative groups. Responsiveness differs between individual patients, ostensibly within the same range of asthma severity, as implied in this and similar studies and may vary with the particular efficacy end point measured. 14 '15 The safety profile of triamcinolone acetonide was similar in all treatment groups. Results of other published clinical studies using HFA as a propellant in aerosol formulations of beclomethasone dipropionate 16 and salbutamol sulfate 17 in adult patients with asthma have also demonstrated comparable safety and efficacy with standard CFC propellantbased products.", "Results of the present study with triamcinolone acetonide confirm those of previous studies, suggesting that HFA-134a is a safe and effective replacement propellant for the currently marketed CFC-containing triamcinolone acetonide inhalation aerosol product. At daily triamcinolone acetonide doses of 150, 300, and 600 ~g, the HFA formulation appears to be safe and effective in improving pulmonary function, relieving asthma symptoms, and reducing the need for supplemental beta-agonist therapy in children with chronic asthma.", "(1 each in HFA 300-~g and P-12 600-~g groups). Both patients were treated with nystatin, and both completed the study."], "domain_risk_levels": ["Some Concerns", "Low Risk", "Low Risk", "Low Risk", "Some Concerns"], "overall_risk_level": "Some Concerns", "paper_id": "29358", "pdf_hash": "18a81009cf1b96fcd175e7d99a764448ede9c64f", "result": [{"addedParagraphs": [], "ctxP1Score": "0.413", "ctxP2Score": "0.299", "ctxP3Score": "0.248", "ctxParagraph1": "Patients not meeting the symptom criteria after the second reduction could not be included in the randomization phase.", "ctxParagraph1Feedback": null, "ctxParagraph2": "To participate in