[{"annotator": "", "document": ["Infections of the skin in children range from common self-limited impetigo to more potentially serious conditions, such as traumatic wound infections. Therapy is usually indicated and must be directed at the causative pathogen. Staphylococcus aureus is a major skin pathogen, but up to 15% of skin infections involve group A beta-haemolytic streptococci. 1, 2 Fusidic acid is highly active against virtually all strains of S. aureus irrespective of their susceptibility to other antibiotics. Although beta-haemolytic streptococci show only moderate susceptibility, 3 fusidic acid is an effective treatment for most skin and soft tissue infections including infections where beta-haemolytic streptococci are implicated. 1, 2 It is particularly important to have an effective antibacterial regimen, which is also safe and well tolerated for use in children. Experience in cutaneous disease and in more deep-seated infection 4, 5 confirm that fusidic acid fulfils these requirements. The suspension formulation is currently administered three times daily, which can be difficult for patients to comply with -for instance, for children at school.", "The absolute bioavailability of fusidic acid suspension is lower than tablets containing sodium fusidate. 6, 7 In a recent pharmacokinetic study, children given a single 10 mg/kg dose of the suspension reached a peak serum concentration of 16.9±7.1 mg/l, which was similar to the levels seen after single oral doses of 250 mg in adults. 8, 9 A convenient regimen of a 250 mg fusidic acid OBJECTIVE: This multicentre, randomized, double-blind, parallel group study aimed to compare a new regimen of fusidic acid suspension against a standard regimen in children with skin and soft tissue infections. METHODS: Treatment groups were given either a new regimen of fusidic acid suspension (20 mg/kg divided BID) or a standard regimen (50 mg/kg divided TID), which were administered for 5 days in both groups and for a further 5 days if evidence of infection persisted. tablet twice daily is now a standard therapy for skin sepsis in adults. 1, 2, 10, 11 Therefore, we conducted a prospective, double-blinded study to compare the efficacy of a twice-daily regimen of fusidic acid suspension (20 mg/kg daily) with the standard 8hourly regimen (50 mg/kg daily) used currently in children with skin and soft tissue infection.", "A prospective, randomized, double-blinded study was conducted at 11 sites in Spain and at 12 sites in Hungary and included children of either sex, aged 1-12 years, with a skin and/or soft tissue infection for which oral antibacterial therapy was indicated. Written informed consent was obtained from the parent(s) or legal guardian, following receipt of verbal and written information about the study.", "The study was designed to comply with the guidelines produced by the ICH and published by the European Agency for Evaluation of Medicinal Products as 'Note for Guidelines on Good Clinical Practice' (CPMP/ICH/ 135/95). The study was conducted to conform with the Declaration of Helsinki as adopted by the 18th World Medical Assembly 1964 and subsequent amendments (Tokyo, Venice, Hong-Kong and South Africa). The study protocol, patient information leaflet and the informed consent form were approved by independent Ethics Committees at each centre.", "Exclusionary criteria included hospital inpatients, children with cellulitis without a focus of infection, chronic or recurrent furunculosis, postoperative wound infection and deep-seated abscesses, history of impaired liver function, diabetes or immunosuppression, those who had received systemic or topical antibacterial therapy in the preceding 7 days and those with an underlying skin condition (e.g. eczema) or were known or suspected to be hypersensitive to the components of fusidic acid suspension. Children previously randomized in the study, participating in another clinical trial, considered unable to comply with the study or who had received an investigational drug in the previous 3 months, as well as girls who had started menstruation were also excluded.", "The children received either 20 mg/kg in two equally divided doses daily or 50 mg/kg in three equally divided doses daily for up 10 days. In the 20 mg/kg group, each of the two doses administered were 125 mg (2.5 ml) for children aged 1-5 years and 250 mg (5 ml) for children aged 6-12 years. In the 50 mg/kg group, the corresponding doses were 250 mg (5 ml) for the 1-5 years age group and 500 mg (10 ml) for 6-12 years. The randomization involved stratification according to whether the lesion was 'open' (e.g. impetigo) or 'closed' (e.g. furuncle).", "Children were evaluated on entry, and days 5 and 10 of treatment and assessed for redness and oedema. Heat was recorded as present or absent and pain (assessed by the patient) as absent, mild, moderate or severe. The presence of a purulent or serous discharge was noted. The overall severity of the lesion was graded as absent, mild, moderate or severe. At the 5-and 10-day assessments the investigator assessed the response to treatment as follows:", "Day 5: 'cured' -lesion healed completely or was sufficiently improved so that no further treatment was required; 'improved' -partial resolution of the lesion but continued treatment with study drug was required; or 'failed' -either (a) no alleviation or worsening of inflammation and signs of infection, (b) surgical drainage of pus necessary after the third day, or (c) treatment discontinued due to inadequate response or adverse event.", "Day 10: 'cured' -lesion healed completely or sufficiently improved so that no further treatment was required; or 'failed' -either (a) partial resolution of the lesion but continued treatment with another drug required, (b) no alleviation or worsening of inflammation and signs of infection, (c) surgical drainage of pus necessary, or (d) treatment discontinued due to inadequate response or adverse event.", "Patients considered 'cured' at the end of treatment were seen for follow-up 2 weeks after completing treatment and the incidence of clinical relapse noted.", "Compliance with the prescribed dosage schedule was recorded daily. A sample of pathogenic material (if present) was taken at all visits and sent to the laboratory for culture. All bacteria cultured were identified. Only S. aureus and beta-haemolytic streptococci were considered pathogenic and their susceptibility to fusidic acid in vitro was determined by a conventional disc testing procedure. A bacteriological response was determined in children infected by fusidic acid-sensitive pathogens and determined as follows: 'cured' -a pathogen on entry and either (a) no pathogen at the end of treatment (eradication) or (b) no swab taken at the end of treatment as no pathological material present (presumptive eradication) or (c) a new, different pathogen present at the end of the treatment; 'failed' -a pathogen on entry and the same pathogen at the end of treatment (persistence).", "End of treatment was defined as the last on-treatment visit.", "Adverse events were elicited by asking a non-leading question. The nature and severity of any adverse events volunteered were recorded and the relationship to study medication assessed. Adverse events considered to be 'possibly' or 'probably' related to fusidic acid were defined as adverse drug reactions.", "The following study populations were defined:", ". safety population -which comprised all patients Fusidic acid for skin and soft tissue infection E To ¨ro ¨k et al randomized to treatment and who provided information concerning tolerance . intention-to-treat population -which comprised all randomized patients who had a skin and/or soft tissue infection for which antibacterial therapy was indicated . per protocol population -which comprised patients in the intention-to-treat population who complied with the protocol inclusion/exclusion criteria . bacteriologically proven population -which comprised patients in the per protocol population who had a proven infection due to S. aureus and/or betahaemolytic streptococci.", "The primary response criterion was defined as the proportion of children considered clinically cured at the end of treatment. Other response criteria included the clinical outcome at 5 days and the clinical relapse rates on follow-up. Bacteriological efficacy was determined in children in the bacteriologically proven population who had an infection due to S. aureus and/or beta-haemolytic streptococci.", "Statistical comparison of proportions was by the chi squared test or Fisher's exact test.", "A total of 445 children, who comprised the safety population, were recruited: 371 in Hungary and 74 in Spain (Table I ). Fifteen patients were excluded from the intention-to-treat population: eight withdrew voluntarily; five were lost to follow-up; one withdrew due to an unacceptable adverse event; and one gave another reason for withdrawal. The intention-to-treat population comprised 430 children: 213 receiving fusidic acid twice daily and 217 receiving fusidic acid three times daily.", "Forty-one patients in the intention-to-treat population violated the protocol procedures: in 32 instances the interval between study visits was too long; three children were older than 12 years; three children took prohibited concurrent medication; two children did not attend visits as planned; and in one patient there was no baseline bacteriology. The per protocol population therefore comprised 389 children: 191 given the twice daily and 198 given the three times daily dosages, respectively. The bacteriologically proven population comprised 331 children: 163 given the twice daily and 168 given the three times daily dosages, respectively (Table II ). The bacteriological response could be assessed in 245 patients: 121 given the twice daily and 124 given the three times daily dosages, respectively (Table III ). In each patient population the two treatment groups statistic showing significance in respect of patient numbers, demographics and diagnostic and prognostic variables. There was no clinically relevant difference between children recruited in Hungary and Spain.", "The clinical response according to the primary response criterion is given in Table II . There were no statistically significant differences in the clinical response at the end of treatment in any patient population assessed. In the intention-to-treat population, both treatments were similarly effective after just 5 days of treatment. Cure was maintained in 177 (92.7%) and in 180 (90.9%) of children in the twice daily and three times daily groups, respectively. Similar results were obtained in the other populations (data not shown). Relapses were all due to bacteriologically proven recurrent impetigo in three and in six children given fusidic acid twice and three times daily, respectively.", "Both suspension regimens were very effective against sensitive pathogens. Eradication was achieved in virtually every instance (Table IV ). There were only two bacteriological failures, both due to failure to eradicate S. aureus from a mixed S. aureus/betahaemolytic streptococcal impetigo in the twice-daily group, where the pathogen isolated at the end of treatment was fusidic acid-resistant and a S. aureus impetigo in the three times daily group, which remained sensitive to fusidic acid throughout. Both cases were also clinical failures.", "Treatment compliance was good, with over 98% of children complying with the prescribed dosage schedule.", "Adverse events and adverse drug reactions, which were 'possibly' or 'probably' treatment-related, are shown in Table V a Children with infection due to fusidic acid-sensitive S. aureus and/or beta-haemolytic streptococci.", "Bacteriological efficacy less common with the new twice-daily regimen (p=0.025). This was due to a significantly greater incidence of gastrointestinal adverse drug reactions seen with the higher dose (p=0.017). Adverse drug reactions caused or contributed to treatment withdrawal in three children given 20 mg/kg and in 12 children given 50 mg/kg (p=0.019), and were the primary reason for stopping treatment in one and in eight children, respectively.", "This study confirms that fusidic acid is an effective and well-tolerated therapy for skin and soft tissue infection in children. Moreover, the twice-daily regimen was comparable with the current three times daily regimen in effectiveness, but with significantly better tolerance.", "Previous studies in adults have shown that the daily dose of fusidic acid can be reduced when treating superficial skin infection, allowing improved tolerance without loss off efficacy. 3 There is now considerable evidence supporting the adoption of twice-daily dosing with fusidic acid in skin and soft tissue infection.", "We treated a large group of children aged 1-12 years, the majority of whom had impetigo, in Spain and Hungary. The study was conducted to the standards required today and produced clear and emphatic conclusions. Study procedures were adhered to well and few children were 'lost to follow-up'. The response criteria were appropriate and the assessments included several clearly defined patient populations. An intention-to-treat population was evaluated and the bacteriological response was assessed in infections due to 'susceptible pathogens', namely S. aureus and beta-haemolytic streptococci. Compliance with treatment was excellent and all outcome measures, clinical and bacteriological, indicated comparable efficacy of the two dosage regimens tested.", "Clinical responses of over 90% were recorded at the end of treatment in all the populations evaluated, including children with a proven infection. The majority of children treated had impetigo. Whilst there were only small numbers of patients with other skin infections, such as acute folliculitis, superficial abscesses and infected traumatic wounds, all responded satisfactorily to fusidic acid. There were few instances where the infection relapsed on follow-up. Similar results have been observed in adults with skin and soft tissue infection given fusidic acid twice daily. 10, 11 The new twice-daily dosage of fusidic acid is suitable for empiric therapy, where treatment has to be given without bacteriological guidance. However, if microbiological facilities are available, providing details of the infecting organism and its susceptibility to fusidic acid, enabling therapy to be targeted specifically, virtually all patients can be treated successfully with the new twice-daily regimen. The bacteriological response approached 100% in infections due to fusidic acid-sensitive S. aureus and beta-haemolytic streptococci. In only one case was a fusidic acid-resistant S. aureus isolated at the end of treatment, confirming the view that the clinical emergence of fusidic acid resistance is uncommon.", "Good tolerance and safety are essential when children are involved. Overall, fusidic acid suspension was well tolerated, with few serious adverse events of concern. The pattern of adverse reactions is similar to that seen previously with oral fusidic acid. 1, 2, 10, 11 As expected, the majority of adverse drug reactions involved gastrointestinal intolerance, which can be reduced significantly and by around three-quarters with the new twice-daily regimen.", "In conclusion, we have shown that twice-daily treatment with fusidic acid suspension is effective both clinically and bacteriologically in children with skin and soft tissue infection. Twice-daily dosing with fusidic acid suspension offers a convenient and well-tolerated dosage schedule. ", "External Dermatological Consultation, Hospital Doce de Octubre, Madrid, Spain;", "External Dermatological Consultation, Hospital Germans Trias I Pujol, Badalona, Spain", "Downloaded by [Gazi University] at 22:47 18 January 2016", "Fusidic acid for skin and soft tissue infection E To ¨ro ¨k et al"], "domain_risk_levels": ["Low Risk", "Low Risk", "Low Risk", "Low Risk", "Low Risk"], "overall_risk_level": "Low Risk", "paper_id": "29643", "pdf_hash": "bfbccdc35866177e5a4fe21be7de5fef30578f9d", "result": [{"addedParagraphs": [], "ctxP1Score": "0.269", "ctxP2Score": "0.256", "ctxP3Score": "0.238", "ctxParagraph1": "The following study populations were defined:", "ctxParagraph1Feedback": null, "ctxParagraph2": ". safety population -which comprised all patients Fusidic acid for skin and soft tissue infection E To ¨ro ¨k et al randomized to treatment and who provided information concerning tolerance . intention-to-treat population -which comprised all randomized patients who had a skin and/or soft tissue infection for which antibacterial therapy was indicated . per protocol population -which comprised patients in the intention-to-treat population who complied with the protocol inclusion/exclusion criteria . bacteriologically proven population -which comprised patients in the per protocol population who had a proven infection due to S. aureus and/or betahaemolytic streptococci.", "ctxParagraph2Feedback": null, "ctxParagraph3": "Adverse events were elicited by asking a non-leading question. The nature and severity of any adverse events volunteered were recorded and the relationship to study medication assessed. Adverse events considered to be 'possibly' or 'probably' related to fusidic acid were defined as adverse drug reactions.", "ctxParagraph3Feedback": null, "domain_number": 1, "expertExplanation": "only information about randomization methods is a statement that the study is randomized", "expertPrediction": "no information", "gptExplanation": "The study population was defined as randomized patients, indicating that the allocation sequence was random.", "gptPrediction": "yes", "gptResponse": "{
