from multiple eligible analyses of the data?"}]}, {"annotator": "V", "document": ["According to international guidelines for pediatric asthma, treatment should be given in a stepwise approach including persistence, severity, and/or frequency of symptoms and should take into account the presenting asthma phenotype (1-3). Children starting a new therapy should be monitored and changes made where appropriate. Recommendations for persistent treatment are based on the randomized controlled trials (4-7). There is a lack of evidence for practical approach to the treatment of newly diagnosed asthma, and they are based mainly on expert opinion (8). This pilot study was aimed at evaluating the effects of different doses of inhaled budesonide and montelukast sodium on clinical symptoms and lung function in children with newly diagnosed asthma.", "Sixty children (26 girls and 34 boys) 5-18 yr of age (mean age 8.2 ± 2.1 yr) with newly", "diagnosed asthma and sensitive only to housedust mites (Dermatophagoides pteronyssimus or/ and Dermatophagoides farinae) were recruited from the outpatient pediatric asthma clinic. Diagnosis of asthma was established by assessing the symptoms of asthma and improvement in the prebronchodilator FEV 1 of ‡12% after administration of salbutamol (200 lg) (2) . For the purposes of the study, skin prick tests (Allergopharma, Germany) for standard allergen extracts were performed; a mean wheal diameter of ‡3 mm at 15 min was defined as a positive response. The main exclusion criteria included treatment during the previous 6 months with an oral, inhaled corticosteroid (ICS). Other exclusion criteria were conditions such as pregnancy, poor compliance with medications. The study was approved by local ethics committee and written consent was obtained from patients and parents.", "This was a randomized, double-blind, parallelgroup, 12-month pilot trial, studying the effects of different doses of inhaled budesonide and montelukast sodium on clinical symptoms and lung function in children with newly diagnosed asthma. Participants received budesonide 800 mcg/day given as dry powder -400 mcg twice daily approximately at 7 am and 7 pm, budesonide 400 mcg/day given as dry powder -200 mcg twice daily approximately at 7 am and 7 pm (Pulmicort Turbuhaler, AstraZeneca, Sweden), montelukast 5 or 10 mg tablet according to age (Singulair, MSD, USA). There were two study visits. The first visit was scheduled from September 2007 to February 2008. They were informed of the aim of the study and were told how to use the inhalers and how to complete The Asthma Therapy Assessment Questionnaire for children (ATAQ) (9) . The Control Domain of ATAQ tool in original version (translated in to our language) was used.", "At the first visit, patients were stratified according to age (6-10 and 11-18 yr) and randomized according to a computer-generated allocation schedule for different treatment with the following: The second visit took place after 6 months of treatment. The subjectÕs ATAQ was evaluated monthly. Also, at the first and second visit, lung function testing in individual patient always took place. Compliance with asthma medication was checked; patients were asked to bring all used and unused medication to each follow-up visit.", "The forced expiratory volume in one second FEV 1 was measured by a spirometer (Jaeger MasterScreenBody; E Jaeger GmbH, Wurzburg, Germany), taking the highest of three successive measurements. Predicted values for all lung function variables were based on a previous study of healthy controls, provided by the lung function test equipment manufacturer (10, 11) .", "The tests were performed according to American Thoracic Society standards (12) .", "The results were analyzed according to wellknown statistical methods by using StatSoft Statistica for Windows, release 6.0 (StatSoft, Inc., Tulsa, OK, USA). Study variables were compared within groups using Wilcoxon test and compared between groups using Kruskal-Wallis test followed by post hoc comparisons with the DunnÕs test. The ATAQ scores were plotted against time for each treatment. The area under the curve (AUC) for the ATAQ scores over the 6-month-treatment period were calculated using a trapezoidal rule. In the next step, newly generated variable -AUC (ATAQ in time) was compared between treatment groups. This strategy allows us to assess the individual effect of treatment over the time.", "All children completed the study; for all patients, ATAQ data concerning all six evaluations were available. Characteristics of the patients who completed the study are given in Table 1 . In 48 (80%) participants, mild symptoms of allergic rhinitis were observed, there were no other comorbidities. After 6 months of treatment with different doses of inhaled budesonide and montelukast sodium, asthma control and lung function significantly improved (within group) (Table 2 ); there were no differences between all three groups of treatment (Fig. 1 , Table 2 ). After first month of treatment, ICS in high dose and montelukast had a stronger effect on total asthma score, than ICS in medium dose (Fig. 1 ). As individual answer to treatment, for each participant, AUC of ATAQ score (points) in time (months of treatment) was calculated and then compared between study groups. There were no significant differences between groups of treatment (Fig. 2 , Table 2 ). We found significant correlation between time of enrollment and individual answer to the treatment in montelukast group only; children enrolled later, at the end of winter season (meaning at the end of heating season) responded better to treatment (Fig. 3a ). The answer to both ICS doses was independent from time of enrollment (Fig. 3b, c ).", "Unexpectedly, 6-month treatment with inhaled steroid in medium dose, inhaled steroid in high dose and montelukast similarly improved control of the newly diagnosed asthma in children. After first month of treatment, ICS in high dose and montelukast had even a stronger effect on total asthma score, than ICS in medium dose. This observation encouraged us to conduct additional analysis to assess individual answer to treatment, which revealed that within montelukast group, children enrolled later into the study, that means 2 or 3 months of treatment during the heating season, responded better to treatment than children who entered at the beginning of heating season. It has been previously shown that natural allergen exposure to dust mites reaches maximum levels during heating season (13, 14) . In our country, heating season meaning the period between September and the end of March. Montelukast, as initial controller monotherapy was more favorable in patients who entered the study later and their treatment went to spring and summer than in children treated during the heating season.", "In conclusion, our study mentioned two viewpoints: first, steroids in medium and even in high dose as initial asthma therapy were no better than montelukast at improving asthma symptoms. Second, natural exposure, even to perennial allergens, should be considered in choosing the initial asthma treatment. We showed that ICS could be a better choice as initial asthma therapy during heating season. The children starting asthma therapy later, at the end of the winter, who are less exposed to allergen than the children starting treatment at the beginning of the winter, have major benefit with montelukast initial monotherapy. It seems, montelukast may be used as sufficient initial asthma monotherapy in children allergic to house-dust mites at the end and out of the heating season. The present investigation was only a pilot study. This study will allow us to calculate minimal sample size for the trial to verify our results."], "domain_risk_levels": ["Low Risk", "Some Concerns", "Low Risk", "Low Risk", "Some Concerns"], "overall_risk_level": "Some Concerns", "paper_id": "29891", "pdf_hash": "9123e3f75afff33b30f4080e487156aa26fd51bb", "result": [