[{"annotator": "", "document": ["A masked, randomized, parallel growth study was conducted in infants fed an amino acid-based formula (AF) or an extensively hydrolyzed casein-based formula (HF). Infants were enrolled between 0 and 9 days and studied to 112 days of age. Growth, formula intake, stool patterns, and serum albumin concentrations were assessed. There were no significant differences between groups in weight, length, or head circumference, gains in weight or length, or study formula intake. The number of stools parents rated as being formed, and the mean daily number of stools were greater in the HF than in the AF group at 14 and 28 days of age. Mean serum albumin concentrations were not significantly different between groups and were within the normal range. This study demonstrates that AF supports normal growth of infants comparable to that of infants fed HF during the critical first 4 months of life.", "Cow milk protein allergy (CMA) is the most common food allergy in early childhood, with an estimated incidence of 2% to 3%. 1, 2 Hypoallergenic formulas based on extensively hydrolyzed casein or whey are recommended for managing formula-fed infants. 3, 4 However, allergic responses can still occur with hypoallergenic hydrolysate formulas because they may contain residual peptides that can elicit an allergic response in sensitive children. [5] [6] [7] An alternative to hydrolysates are formulas based on free amino acids. Such formulas are indicated for infants with extreme protein hypersensitivity, for infants whose symptoms persist even with the feeding of extensively hydrolyzed formulas, and for infants with CMA who have failure-to-thrive. [8] [9] [10] [11] Although studies have demonstrated that hypoallergenic amino acid-based formulas are efficacious for treating symptoms in virtually all infants with CMA, few studies have focused on the efficacy of these formulas in supporting normal growth during infancy.", "Most studies of amino acid-based formulas were conducted in relatively small populations of infants with CMA or intolerance, and most studies were not designed to measure effects on growth. 7, [12] [13] [14] [15] [16] [17] [18] [19] The present study was designed to assess the growth of healthy term infants fed an amino acid-based formula versus an extensively hydrolyzed casein-based formula in a randomized controlled trial that met robust criteria proposed 20 for evaluating the ability of a formula to support growth of infants.", "A masked, randomized, parallel growth study (ClinicalTrials.gov Identifier NCT01699386) was conducted. Infants were randomly assigned to receive 1 of 2 formulas, a hypoallergenic amino acid-based formula (AF) or a hypoallergenic extensively hydrolyzed caseinbased infant formula (HF). Formulas were fed from enrollment until 112 days of age. Parents were instructed to feed the assigned formula as the sole source of nutrition ad libitum for the duration of the study. The primary objective was to compare weight gain from 14 to 112 days of age, and weight at each study visit. Length, head circumference (HC), length gain, study formula intake, daily stool number, mean rank stool consistency (MRSC), and serum albumin were also measured.", "A pseudo-random permuted-blocks computer algorithm prepared by the sponsor equally randomized treatments within gender at each site. Formula was identified by code only and study personnel, parents and caregivers were unaware of formula identity. Packaging for both formulas was similar. Blinding was not broken until after all data had been collected.", "Healthy, singleton term infants with birth weights ≥2500 g were enrolled between 0 and 9 days of age and studied to 112 days of age. Parents agreed to use the study formulas as the sole source of nutrition and to not administer any mineral or vitamin supplements during the study. Water was permitted ad libitum. Exclusion criteria included maternal, fetal, or perinatal medical conditions with the potential for adverse effects on growth, development, or both, such as diabetes (gestational diabetes was acceptable if the infant's birth weight was less than the 95th percentile), tuberculosis, intrauterine infections, or suspected substance abuse. ", "Both formulas provided levels of nutrients recommended by the American Academy of Pediatrics Committee on Nutrition, and were in compliance with the Infant Formula Act. 21, 22 The AF formula was EleCare Amino Acid-Based Medical Food (Abbott Nutrition, Abbott Laboratories, Columbus, OH); the HF formula was Nutramigen Protein Hydrolysate Infant Formula (Mead Johnson Nutritionals, Evansville, IN). Both formulas were in powdered form and provided 20 kcal/fl oz when fed at standard dilution.", "The macronutrient contents of both formulas were similar but the sources of macronutrients were different (Table 1 ).", "Study visits occurred when infants were were made at enrollment and at each study visit, and were plotted against the World Health Organization (WHO) growth standards. 23 Parents completed 3-day intake records before each study visit, and 3-day stool records before the 14-and 28-day visits. These records included the characteristics (consistency and frequency) of the infant's stools and volume of formula consumed at each feeding. For calculation of MRSC, each stool was ranked by the parents on a scale of 1 to 5 (1 = watery to 5 = hard). The mean stool rank was calculated for each day, and the mean of these daily means was calculated over the study period and is reported as the average MRSC. Blood samples (approximately 0.75 mL) were collected by heel stick or venipuncture at the 112-day visit for measurement of serum albumin. Samples were centrifuged and serum was frozen and stored at -20°C until shipment to a central laboratory (Quest Diagnostics, Inc, San Juan Capistrano, CA), where albumin concentration was measured by the bromocresol green method.", "A sample size of 64 infants in each group would have 80% power to detect a difference in weight gain of 3 g/d from day 14 to 112, assuming that the common standard deviation is 6 g/d using a 2-group t test with a .05 twosided significance level. Weight gain and length gain per day were calculated as the difference between the initial and the final measurement divided by the number of elapsed days. Weight gain (g/d) and length gain (mm/d) from 14 to 112 days were compared using analysis of variance (ANOVA). The factors in the model were feeding group and sex. Weight, length, and HC were compared using repeated measures ANOVA with the SAS procedure PROC MIXED. The factors in the model were feeding group, sex, and their interactions. The MRSC was analyzed using ANOVA with a random blocking factor for site, and the average daily number of stools and serum albumin concentrations were analyzed by ANOVA. All tests were 2-sided, and results were considered statistically significant if the P value was less than .05. The study results are shown as mean ± the standard error of the mean and are for the intent-to-treat group unless otherwise indicated.", "A total of 213 infants were enrolled into the study and randomized (Figure 1 ). Of these, 134 infants completed the study whereas 79 infants left the study early.", "The demographic characteristics including gender, gestational age, ethnicity, age at study entry, birth weight, length, HC, and weight at study entry were similar between groups (Table 2 ).", "There were no statistically significant differences between feeding groups in mean weight (Table 3 ) or in mean weight gain, which was 28.2 ± 0.7 (n = 73) and 28.2 ± 0.6 g/d (n = 81) for the AF and HF groups, respectively (P = .8670, 95% confidence interval = -2.2 to 1.9). The results of the per protocol analysis were similar to those of the intent-to-treat analysis. Mean weight for males and females are shown in comparison with the WHO growth standards in Figure 2 . There were no statistically significant differences between the groups for length or HC (Table 3 ) or for length gain (data not shown) during the study.", "Mean study formula intakes for the groups were similar at 14, 28, 56, 84, and 112 days of age (Table 4 ).", "There were statistically significant differences in stool patterns between groups. The average MRSC and mean daily number of stools were significantly greater in the HF group at 14 and 28 days of age (Table 4 ).", "At 112 days of age, the mean serum albumin concentration was not statistically different between groups (42.2 ± 0.3 and 43.3 ± 0.4 g/L for the AF [n = 59] and HF [n = 68] groups, respectively, P = .0513, 95% confidence interval = -2.0 to 0.2).", "No safety concerns emerged during this study.", "We report that the growth of infants fed a hypoallergenic amino acid-based formula (AF) was similar to that of infants fed a hypoallergenic extensively hydrolyzed casein-based formula (HF). Infants started to receive study formulas at a mean age of approximately 6 days and continued until 112 days of age; 65 and 69 infants completed the study in the AF and HF groups, respectively. There were no significant differences between groups in the rates of gain in weight or length, or in weight, length, or HC during the study. The mean weight of males and females was close to the 50th percentile of the WHO standards, indicating a normal growth pattern. Serum albumin levels at 112 days of age were similar between groups and were within the normal range. Our results are in agreement with those of other investigators who reported normal growth patterns in infants fed HF. [24] [25] [26] However, only a few studies assessed the growth of normal infants fed amino acid-based formulas as a primary outcome, and some did not meet rigorous standards for clinical evaluation of infant growth. The US Food and Drug Administration, 20 with guidance from the American Academy of Pediatrics Committee on Nutrition, 27 proposed rules for evaluating the ability of formulas to support growth of infants, including that (a) the evaluation should be made when the formulas are used as the sole source of nutrition; (b) the rate of weight gain should be determined over an interval of 3 to 4 months; (c) that growth assessment should include the first 8 weeks of life because nutrient requirements per kilogram of body weight are greatest during this period; (d) that growth measurements should be made at the beginning of the study, 2 and 4 weeks later, and at least monthly thereafter until conclusion of the study; and (e) that studies need to have sufficient statistical power to detect a feeding-related difference in weight gain of more than 3 g/d over a 3-to 4-month period, which is considered to be nutritionally relevant. The number of subjects of a specified sex needed in each of 2 groups to detect this difference at P < .05 with a power of 80% in a 1-tailed test is 28.", "Although the published studies concerning amino acid-based formulas report that they supported the growth of infants, 7, [12] [13] [14] [15] [16] [17] 28 growth was not the primary outcome variable for most studies. In a well-designed growth study, Burks et al 24 reported that healthy, term infants fed an amino acid-based formula or HF had similar rates of growth. However, most studies of amino acid-based formulas involved infants with established allergy or intolerance to cow milk, and such infants often have multiple confounding clinical factors, including growth restriction, and the studies therefore did not meet the more stringent criteria for evaluating the ability of a formula to support the growth of normal infants. 7, [12] [13] [14] [15] [17] [18] [19] The evaluation of growth was the primary outcome variable of the present study, which met the stringent criteria for evaluation of infant growth.", "In the present study, 42 infants fed AF and 37 fed HF exited from the study early, with 25% fed AF and 20% fed HF exiting because of intolerance symptoms. In our experience, these attrition rates are typical of long-term studies with extensively hydrolyzed or amino acid-based formula when fed to healthy term infants. When these formulas are fed to intended populations, attrition rates are dramatically lower. These formulas are noticeably different from standard formulas in appearance, taste, and smell and because of their elemental nature may modify typical feeding and stooling patterns of healthy infants. We speculate that parents of healthy term infants are less tolerant of these differences than are parents of sick infants where, in fact, feeding and stooling patterns of their infant may normalize on these formulas.", "A strength of our study is that tolerance and formula intake were assessed systematically. Parents completed 3-day intake records before the visits at 14, 28, 56, 84, and 112 days, and they completed 3-day stool records before the visits at 14 and 28 days. The records included 24-hour intake data and detailed information about the consistency of each stool. The volume of formula consumed was similar between groups throughout the study. However, infants fed the HF had significantly more stools that parents rated as firm or hard, and a significantly greater number of stools per day than infants fed AF. This difference in stooling pattern and consistency is likely because of the use of palm olein oil as a source of fat in the HF fed in this study. Lloyd et al 29 reported that formula with palm olein oil is associated with firmer stools.", "Although extensively hydrolyzed formulas and amino acid-based formulas are effective in relieving symptoms in most infants with CMA, amino acid-based formulas are preferable for certain patients. [8] [9] [10] [11] Hill et al 10 reported that specific subgroups of infants have better resolution of symptoms on amino acid-based formulas than on extensively hydrolyzed formula, and they more often have catch-up growth. The subgroups include infants with non-IgE mediated food-induced gastroenterocolitis-proctitis syndromes with failure-to-thrive, those with severe atopic eczema, and those with symptoms during exclusive breast-feeding. Isolauri et al 14 reported that growth of infants with CMA was improved in infants fed an amino acid-based formula but not in infants fed an extensively hydrolyzed whey-based formula, and concluded that an amino acid-based formula is preferable for infants with multiple food allergies, especially because it maintains normal growth. Niggemann et al 17 reported that atopic dermatitis improved in infants with CMA who were fed an amino acid-based formula but remained constant in those fed an extensively hydrolyzed whey-based formula. Other investigators report that infants with CMA did not react to amino acid-based formula, but some infants react to extensively hydrolyzed formula with bloody stools, vomiting, diarrhea, irritability, eczema, and failure to gain weight. 5, 19 Caution is needed, however, in comparing studies of amino acid-based formulas with studies of hydrolyzed formulas. Clinical outcomes can be affected by other differences in the composition of formulas, including hydrolysate source, the extent of hydrolysis, fat blend with possible effects on energy availability, amino acid blend, protein content, and fortification with other nutrients.", "The present robust study meets rigorous criteria for the evaluation of infant formulas, and demonstrates that this amino acid-based formula supports normal growth of infants during the critical first 4 months of life. As the incidence of food allergy continues to increase, 1 the use of amino acid-based formulas will likely increase, especially for infants with severe CMA and for specific subsets of infants whose symptoms are resolved more effectively with amino acid-based formulas than with extensively hydrolyzed formulas.", "Abbreviations: AF, amino acid-based formula; HF, extensively hydrolyzed casein-based formula."], "domain_risk_levels": ["Some Concerns", "Low Risk", "Low Risk", "Low Risk", "Low Risk"], "overall_risk_level": "Some Concerns", "paper_id": "30365", "pdf_hash": "101ec0ca994404e149c35b8b908ee9b439291d17", "result": [{"addedParagraphs": [], "ctxP1Score": "0.379", "ctxP2Score": "0.336", "ctxP3Score": "0.282", "ctxParagraph1": "A pseudo-random permuted-blocks computer algorithm prepared by the sponsor equally randomized treatments within gender at each site. Formula was identified by code only and study personnel, parents and caregivers were unaware of formula identity. Packaging for both formulas was similar. Blinding was not broken until after all data had been collected.", "ctxParagraph1Feedback": "good", "ctxParagraph2": "A total of 213 infants were enrolled into the study and randomized (Figure 1 ). Of these, 134 infants completed the study whereas 79 infants left the study early.", "ctxParagraph2Feedback": "bad", "ctxParagraph3": "Healthy, singleton term infants with birth weights ≥2500 g were enrolled between 0 and 9 days of age and studied to 112 days of age. Parents agreed to use the study formulas as the sole source of nutrition and to not administer any mineral or vitamin supplements during the study. Water was permitted ad libitum. Exclusion criteria included maternal, fetal, or perinatal medical conditions with the potential for adverse effects on growth, development, or both, such as diabetes (gestational diabetes was acceptable if the infant's birth weight was less than the 95th percentile), tuberculosis, intrauterine infections, or suspected substance abuse. ", "ctxParagraph3Feedback": "bad", "domain_number": 1, "expertExplanation": "The study mentions that a pseudo-random permuted-blocks computer algorithm prepared by the sponsor was used to equally randomize treatments within gender at each site. Therefore, the allocation sequence was random.", "expertPrediction": "yes", "gptExplanation": "The study mentions that a pseudo-random permuted-blocks computer algorithm prepared by the sponsor was used to equally randomize treatments wi