multiple eligible outcome measurements (e.g. scales, definitions, time points) within the outcome domain?"}, {"addedParagraphs": [], "ctxP1Score": "0.261", "ctxP2Score": "0.236", "ctxP3Score": "0.209", "ctxParagraph1": "A sample size of 64 infants in each group would have 80% power to detect a difference in weight gain of 3 g/d from day 14 to 112, assuming that the common standard deviation is 6 g/d using a 2-group t test with a .05 twosided significance level. Weight gain and length gain per day were calculated as the difference between the initial and the final measurement divided by the number of elapsed days. Weight gain (g/d) and length gain (mm/d) from 14 to 112 days were compared using analysis of variance (ANOVA). The factors in the model were feeding group and sex. Weight, length, and HC were compared using repeated measures ANOVA with the SAS procedure PROC MIXED. The factors in the model were feeding group, sex, and their interactions. The MRSC was analyzed using ANOVA with a random blocking factor for site, and the average daily number of stools and serum albumin concentrations were analyzed by ANOVA. All tests were 2-sided, and results were considered statistically significant if the P value was less than .05. The study results are shown as mean ± the standard error of the mean and are for the intent-to-treat group unless otherwise indicated.", "ctxParagraph1Feedback": null, "ctxParagraph2": "There were no statistically significant differences between feeding groups in mean weight (Table 3 ) or in mean weight gain, which was 28.2 ± 0.7 (n = 73) and 28.2 ± 0.6 g/d (n = 81) for the AF and HF groups, respectively (P = .8670, 95% confidence interval = -2.2 to 1.9). The results of the per protocol analysis were similar to those of the intent-to-treat analysis. Mean weight for males and females are shown in comparison with the WHO growth standards in Figure 2 . There were no statistically significant differences between the groups for length or HC (Table 3 ) or for length gain (data not shown) during the study.", "ctxParagraph2Feedback": null, "ctxParagraph3": "The demographic characteristics including gender, gestational age, ethnicity, age at study entry, birth weight, length, HC, and weight at study entry were similar between groups (Table 2 ).", "ctxParagraph3Feedback": null, "domain_number": 5, "expertExplanation": "The numerical result being assessed (weight gain) was not selected from multiple eligible analyses of the data. The study specifically mentioned the statistical tests conducted and showed that there were no statistically significant differences between the feeding groups in terms of weight gain.", "expertPrediction": "no", "gptExplanation": "The numerical result being assessed (weight gain) was not selected from multiple eligible analyses of the data. The study specifically mentioned the statistical tests conducted and showed that there were no statistically significant differences between the feeding groups in terms of weight gain.", "gptPrediction": "no", "gptResponse": "{
