[{"annotator": "", "document": ["Infant formula design aim^s at achieving the same beneficial effects on physical and physiological development seen in breastfed itifants. The objective of this study was to evaluate the safety of an infant milk formula supplemented with GOS (OAgllOOmL) in terms of growth and incidence of adverse effects. This was a double blind, multicenter, parallel group, randomized clinical trial Weight (g), length (cm.), and occipito frontal head circumference (cm) were measured. The incidence of adverse events (AEs) and infants acceptance and tolerance of the study formula were evaluated. A total of 199 breast fed infants and 163 formula fed (80 in the control and 83 in the study group) were recruited. All three groups showed appropriate physical development throughout the period. Length was significantly associated with breastfeeding. Diarrhea was comparably present in all groups. GOS supplemented formula stimulated infants' growth and prevented AEs similarly to human milk.", "Breast feeding is considered as the reference standard for infant feeding and nutrition (WHO and UNICEF 2003, 2012) . Human milk provides high nutritional and protective degree, mainly due to its nutritional composition that changes over the lactation period and to the biological activities of specific components; proteins, lipids, carbohydrates, and other bioactive components in human milk provide essential nutrients and energy; they also function to support growth and development in other ways. Categories of bioactive compounds in human milk include immune factors (such as secretory IgA, or sIgA), hormones, growth factors, proteins and peptides, lipids, carbohydrates, and other components (Mountzouris, McCartney et al. 2002 ). When breastfeeding is not possible, infant formulae are the only substitutes. Research on infanr formula design aimed at achieving the same beneficial effects on physical and physiological development seen in breast-fed infants. In particular, when taking into consideration the gastrointestinal (GI) microflora breast milk contains some agents that exert a boosting effect on the growth and activity of commensal bacteria, mainly of Bifidobacteria (Palmer, Bik et al. 2007) , and oligosaccharides which inhibit pathogens colonization (Newburg 2000) . Oligosaccharides that are contained in human milk are considered to be the prototype of prebiotics, since they have been shown to facilitate the growth of Bifidobacteria and Lactobacilli in the colon of breast-fed neonates (Szajewska and Makrides 2011) . Prebiotics addition to infant formulae has grown in diffusion in the latest year, principally focusing on supplementation of a mixture of Fructo-oligosaccharides (FOS) and Galactooligosaccharides (GOS) (Knol, Boehm et al. 2005) . The Committee on Nutrition ofthe European Society for Paediatric Gastroenterology, Hepatology, and Nutrition (ESPGHAN) has recently concluded the available scientific data suggest that the administration of currently evaluated probiotic-supplemented formula to healthy infants does not raise safety concerns with regard to growth and adverse effects (Braegger, Chmielewska et al. 2011) . Up to now, only few studies have analyzed the growth of infants fed with a formula supplemented only with GOS (Ben, Zhou et al. 2004 , Ben, Li et al. 2008) . The primary objective of this study was to evaluate the safety of an infant milk formula supplemented with GOS (0.4g/100mL) in terms of growth and incidence of adverse effects.", "This was a double blind, multicenter, parallel group, randomized clinical trial performed by 6 centers in Italy between February, 2006 and October, 2011. Trial profile is summarized in Figure 1 . The study was conducted in compliance with the Declaration of Helsinki (version revised Tokyo 2004), the Good Chnical Practices (CPMP/ICH/135/95) and in accordance with national regulatory requirements and laws. The Ethics Committees of each participating center approved all relevant study documents. All patients provided signed informed consent and could withdraw from the study at any time. This trial is registered with ClinicalTrials.gov, number NCT00486148.", "Healthy term infants, born through natural or Caesarean delivery were enrolled within 15 days from birth. All inclusion and exclusion criteria are listed in Table 1 .", "Three groups were considered according to the type of feeding: breast milk, GOS supplemented infant milk formula (study formula) and standard infant milk formula (control formula). ", "Formula-fed (FF) infants were randomized to receive either the control or the study formula and constime the assigned formtila exclusively until the introduction of complementary feeding. The compositions of the two formulae were identical, apart from the supplemented oligosaccharides. The study formula was supplemented with 0.4g/100 mL of GOS (Vivinal' GOS Galactooligosaccharide; Friesland Foods Domo). Study and control formtila were supplied by Heinz Italia S.p.A. (Latina, Italy). The two formtdae were provided in powdered form and could not be differentiated by smell, consistency or any other characteristics. Neithet the investigators nor the parents knew which product the infant was receiving.", "In order to assess the growth, the following anthropométrie parameters were measured: weight (g), length (cm), and occipito-frontal head circumference (cm). Infants were weighed without a diaper on a high precision electronic gram scales, and measurements were recorded to the neatest gram. The scales were balanced and calibrated before the trial and at intervals for the duration of the study. Length was measured on recumbent length boards and head circumference was assessed through a flexible measuring tape with. This method has been previously validated (Peerson, Heinig et al. 1993, Chierici and Vigi 1994) and used in other clinical trials on infant formulas (Bettler and Euler 2006) . We examined 3 z scores as indicators of infant growth: weight-for-age, length-for-age and weight-for-length.", "The use of z scores allows statistical comparison between the sample and current growth references and standardizes weight and length data for what is considered normative for each infants gender and age at measurement. Z scores were compared with WHO Child Growth Standards(de Onis, Onyango et al. 2006) .", "The incidence of adverse events (AEs) and infants acceptance and tolerance of the study formula were evaluated. AEs were defined as undesired, unplanned clinical event, in the form of signs and symptoms, or disease, occurring in an infant participating in a clinical study, regardless of causal relationship, like diarrhea, infantile colics, prolonged crying, régurgitation, vomiting, cutaneous manifestations, fever. The assessment of the severity of the AEs and whether the AEs were considered to be related to the study formula were determined by the investigators at each site. AEs were recorded based on findings from the investigators during the physical examinations and clinical evaluations of the infant and on the data recorded by parents in a diary.", "Anthropométrie parameters and any adverse events (AEs) were recorded at six different time points: baseline visit (B) within 15 days after birth, visit 1 (V') (30 ± 10 days of life), visit 2 CV") (60 ± 10 days of life), visit 3 (V) (90 ± 10 days of life), visit 4 (V) (120 ±10 days of life) and final visit (V), performed just before the introduction of complementary feeding. A fecal sample was collected at B, V V^and V^and quali-quantitative analyses were performed in order to assess Bifidobacteria, Clostridium, Lactobacilli, Escherichia coli presence and count. In presence of adverse effects, an extra visit was planned and a fecal sample collected. At B, parents were given a diary to daily record stool frequency and consistency and adverse events. Infants' growth was assessed through anthropométrie measurements of weight (g), length (cm), and occipito-frontal head circumference (cm) and z-scores were calculated.", "A total of 160 formulas fed infants (80 receiving the control formula and 80 receiving the study one) would give the power to detect a 24% reduction in adverse events for the study formula as compared with the standard one, at the 80% power and 5% significance level, assuming a 20% drop out rate. Recruiting 200 breast fed infants, assuming a 20% drop out rate and a breast feeding rate at 6 months at about 50%, would ensure a control group of 64 exclusively breast fed infants at 6 months.", "Data have been presented using median (I and III quartile as measures of dispersion) for quantitative variables and as percentages (absolute numbers) for categorical variables. We used World Health Organization (WHO) Anthro 3.0.1 software (available at www.who.int/ childgrowth/en) to calculate age-and gender-specific z scores on the basis of the WHO growth standards, which use the breastfed child as the norm. Second, we used Epi Info 3.5.1 software (available at www.cdc.gov/epiinfo) to calculate age-and gender-specific z scores on the basis of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention growth references (Mennella, Ventura et al. 2011) . Tests for the difference of main variables at each visit was performed using a test statistic based on Chi-square test and on Wilcoxon or Kruskal-Wallis test using the F distribution, whenever appropriate. Risk rates of adverse events were based on a Poisson log-linear model, treating child-days of exposure as an offset in the model. Growth curves have been estimated using a restricted cubic spline smoother implemented on a robustified OLS model. In case of multiple assessment per subject and for growth curve estimation, p-values are derived by the estimation of a robustified version of the above mentioned statistics, using the Huber-White method (Huber 1967 ). All analyses have been performed using R system (Team 2012) .", "A total of 199 breast fed infants and 163 formula fed (80 in the control group and 83 in the study group) were recruited, with a dropout rate of 39% and 26%, respectively. Voluntary interruption was the most common drop out reason in all groups, respectively 58% in the breast fed group and 86% in the formula one. Table 2 presents the general characteristics of the sample, based on the delivery method. Groups were well matched at baseline, with maternal characteristics and gestational age comparable between the groups. Female and male infants were equally represented in both groups, and the majority of the infants (53%, 187 infants) were first-born children. Higher 44) 52 ( 45) 43 ( 37) 5( 4) 38 (38-39) 59 (54-69) 72 (66-82) 163 (160-165) 29 ( 25) 49 ( 30) 31( 31) 22( 12) 0(0) 30( 16)", "2( 1) 46 ( 25 gestational age was significantly associated with natural delivery. Maternal choice was the main reason to opt for baby formula instead of breast milk (48% of overall cases), whilst agalactia was the second most frequent reason (26%). When considering the general data, a significant association (p<0.001) was found between natural delivery and the choice to breast-feed.", "A separate evaluation of anthropometries data collected was performed, considering three different groups divided by type of feeding (human milk, control formula, study formula). Anthropometries were registered at Baseline, after 30, 60, 90, 120 days of life and just before the introduction of complementary feeding. All data are reported in Table 3 . All three groups showed appropriate physical development throughout the period. Neither weights nor head circumference of infants differed between the groups nor subgroups at birth nor in the follow up period, while length was significantly associated with breast-feeding. All parameters were adequate with respect with WHO reference ranges (Kuczmarski, Ogden et al. 2000) . Over 5 months, the median weight in breast-fed increased of 3850 g, while in infants fed with control and study formula increased of 3900 g and 4200 g, respectively. Median infant length increased 14.5 cm breast-fed, 14 cm in the control and 9.5 cm in the study group. Head circumference enlarged of 8 cm in breast-fed infants, 7 cm in in the control and 8 cm in the study group, a As illustrated in Figures 2, 3 and 4, supplemented formula fed infants showed a superimposable growth development with breast fed infants. No significant differences were found in anthropometries variables, with except of length in breast fed infants.", "Safety of the formula was also assessed comparing the number and type of AEs. Diarrhea was comparably present in all groups, with a higher number of discharges in the control formula. Breast fed children showed a significant higher frequency of stools and consistency. These were the only two", "Any dietary component that reaches the colon intact is a potential prebiotic. Most of the interest in the development of prebiotics is focused on non-digestible oligosaccharides. Our study was aimed at assessing the safety of a GOS supplemented formula, comparing a group of infants fed with the supplemented formula, not only with a group fed with standard formula, but also with a group of breast fed infants. The results of our study confirmed the initial hypothesis, showing that the three groups exhibit similar growth trends and acceptance of the chosen feeding method within the follow-up period. Several studies have been trying to prove the prebioric effect of FOS and GOS supplementation in infant formulae, but at present, there is no general consensus on their potential in mimicking the beneficial effects on human milk oligosaccharides (Braegger, Chmielewska et al. 2011) . Although comparable, that, as already seen in Chouraqui's study (Chouraqui, Grathwohl et al. 2008) , prebiotic-supplemented formulae are safe for infant development. A potential negative aspect in formula is often said to be the initial fast effect on growth, which is not matched by similar long time effect (Whitehead and Paul 1984) . Our study showed that not only infants fed with the study formula developed in a comparable way to the breast fed ones, but also that the effect could be seen in a prolonged period. Several trials have been assessing the effect of different proportions of FOS and GOS (Ziegler, Vanderhoof et al. 2007 , Rao, Srinivasjois et al. 2009 ) as well as GOS supplementation (Ben, Zhou et al. 2004 , Rao, Srinivasjois et al. 2009 ), but the relevance of reported outcome measures is still not clear (Sherman, Cabana et al. 2009) . Ben and colleagues pointed out the beneficial effect of GOS in stool frequency and consistency, infiuenced by the supplement, and resulting in a significantly increased frequency in infants fed with GOS supplemented formula and human milk (Ben, Zhou et al. 2004 ). Our study highlighted that GOS supplemented formula can be considered safe both in tetms of nutritional adequacy for proper development and incidence of adverse effects. Softer stools were recorded in GOS supplemented formula fed infants in a comparable way to breast fed ones, implying a lower incidence of adverse effects linked to hard stools and constipation (Quinlan, Lockton et al. 1995) . Flatulence episodes did not show any significant difference among the groups, confirming the safety of the developed formula. When considering the other AEs, figures could be matched. GOS supplemented formula showed a significant association (p<0.05) with the consistency of stools, indicating once again a performance similar to breast milk. Many studies have shown that when infants were fed with formulae supplemented with prebiotics, their stool consistency and pattern were similar to those of breast-fed infants (Ziegler, Vanderhoof et al. 2007 ). In our study infants showed lower frequencies of watery stools with respect to human milk fed infants, while soft and normal stools were comparable between the two groups. One of the goals of our study was also to compare the petformance of the GOS supplemented formula towards the control formula. In summary, in our study GOS supplemented formula stimulated infants' growth and prevented AEs similarly to human milk."], "domain_risk_levels": ["Some Concerns", "Some Concerns", "Low Risk", "Low Risk", "Low Risk"], "overall_risk_level": "Some Concerns", "paper_id": "30495", "pdf_hash": "0837a3b2aea1b896228591d30e7d5e3760e5e96a", "result": [{"addedParagraphs": [], "ctxP1Score": "0.229", "ctxP2Score": "0.226", "ctxP3Score": "0.215", "ctxParagraph1": "This was a double blind, multicenter, parallel group, randomized clinical trial performed by 6 centers in Italy between February, 2006 and October, 2011. Trial profile is summarized in Figure 1 . The study was conducted in compliance with the Declaration of Helsinki (version revised Tokyo 2004), the Good Chnical Practices (CPMP/ICH/135/95) and in accordance with national regulatory requirements and laws. The Ethics Committees of each participating center approved all relevant study documents. All patients provided signed informed consent and could withdraw from the study at any time. This trial is registered with ClinicalTrials.gov, number NCT00486148.", "ctxParagraph1Feedback": "bad", "ctxParagraph2": "Healthy term infants, born through natural or Caesarean delivery were enrolled within 15 days from birth. All inclusion and exclusion criteria are listed in Table 1 .", "ctxParagraph2Feedback": "bad", "ctxParagraph3": "A total of 199 breast fed infants and 163 formula fed (80 in the control group and 83 in the study group) were recruited, with a dropout rate of 39% and 26%, respectively. Voluntary interruption was the most common drop out reason in all groups, respectively 58% in the breast fed group and 86% in the formula one. Table 2 presents the general characteristics of the sample, based on the delivery method. Groups were well matched at baseline, with maternal characteristics and gestational age comparable between the groups. Female and male infants were equally represe