[{"annotator": "", "document": ["Objective: This study was designed with objective to study pain response of infants to change in sequence of administration of Hepatitis B and DTwP vaccines. Methods: This was a randomized parallel control trial. The study was carried out in the immunization clinic of the Department of Pediatrics, LLRM Medical College, Meerut. One hundred and thirty healthy term infants up to 4 months of age were injected either DTwP vaccine first or Hepatitis B vaccine first, followed one minute later by the other vaccine. Result: Baseline characteristics did not differ between the groups. The mean (SD) of AUC of MFCS and NIPS was significantly more in DF group as compared to HF group (for MFCS 25.5 ± 5.4 versus 22.5 ± 5.5, p < 0.01; for NIPS 31.77 ± 5.5 versus 27.64 ± 6.9, p < 0.01). Similarly mean (SD) of AUC of Heart rate and saturation of oxygen showed significant variation in DF group as compared to HF group (for heart rate 591.6 ± 55 versus 559.6 ± 49, p < 0.01; for SpO 2 326.4 ± 12 versus 335 ± 8, p < 0.01). Conclusion: These results showed that infant experienced lesser pain when Hepatitis B was administered first than when DTwP vaccine was given first.", "Vaccination is the most common procedure causing undue pain in an infant. It is associated with increased anxiety, avoidance and parent distress [29] . Although it may not be possible to completely eliminate pain in infants, measures to reduce the amount and intensity of pain is important not only because it is an ethical parental expectation but also because repeated painful exposures can have deleterious consequences [4, 10, 27] . Growing scientific and clinical evidence from both animal and human infant points to the efficacy of natural, non-pharmacologic interventions in reducing pain due to minor procedures [5] . Although effective pain relief is now usually provided for infants during and after a major surgical procedure, pain-reducing therapies are often underused for the numerous minor procedures like vaccination that are a part of routine medical care for infants [12] . With the advent of more and more vaccines, multiple inoculations in a visit is a reality despite more vaccines now being available as combinations. In addition, numerous non-pharmacological and pharmacological intervention, simple sequence of vaccine administration is a procedural technique that can potentially reduce pain in infants. Studies with some vaccine have shown that their co-administration in a particular order result in reduced pain perception [15, 24] . Though Hepatitis B first dose is recommended at 4 week and DTwP at 6 week, they are often given together at 6 weeks. This happens if Hepatitis B was delayed in first or second dose due to any reason. Though hexavalent vaccine with DPT/HiB/Hepatitis B is available in developed countries, it is not available in India. So they have to be given separately. We, therefore, designed a study to evaluate if the sequence or order of administration of first DTwP and Hepatitis B has impact on perception of pain in young infants up to 4 months of age.", "This study was designed as a randomized parallel group active controlled trial. After approval from Institutional Ethical Committee, this study was carried out in the Immunization Clinic of Department of Pediatrics, LLRM Medical College, Meerut, India.", "Trial Registration: This trial was registered at clinical trial registry of India (CTRI/2014/11/005207).", "Healthy full term infants, up to 4 months of postnatal age, were enrolled who attended immunization clinic for first DTwP vaccine and any dose of Hepatitis B vaccine as per the immunization program. Exclusion criteria were: infants who were admitted in hospital for more than 48 h, perinatal asphyxia (1 min Apgar score <7) or delayed cry (duration >5 min) if born at home, small for gestational age, pre-term (<37 week of gestation), previous surgery or any major congenital anomaly.", "Infants were randomly allocated to 1 of 2 groups in a 1:1 ratio by block randomization with computer-generated randomly permuted blocks of 8. The numbers were written on small slips and placed in sequentially numbered opaque sealed envelopes (SNOSE METHOD). Random sequence was generated by an independent statistician using online software generated random number list with URL http://www.sealedenvelope.com. The subjects were randomized into two groups:", "(i) HF group-Hepatitis B vaccination prior to DTwP vaccination. (ii) DF group-DTwP vaccination prior to Hepatitis B vaccination.", "The sealed envelopes were opened in a separate room by the staff on duty. The staff loaded the two syringes with DTwP and Hepatitis B vaccine and labeled 'first' and 'second' according to order in the slip over opaque tape. The doctor received two labeled syringes and injected vaccines in order, firstly injecting vaccine labeled as 'first' and one minute later the other vaccine labeled as 'second'.", "After explaining the full procedure, written consent was taken from caretakers in their local language. Babies were brought to the room where vaccination was to be done. Infants were comfortably placed on immunization table in supine position. The demographics, clinical characteristics and hemodynamic parameters of all India) or Hepatitis B (Biological E. limited, S.P Biotech Park, Andhra Pradesh, India). One minute later, the other vaccine was administered on opposite thigh. During the process of injecting the vaccine, the needle was inserted intramuscularly at 90 • to the skin surface with steady pressure. No aspiration was performed [28] and the vaccine material was rapidly injected over 1-2 s followed by rapid withdrawal of the needle [8] . During this procedure, vaccine injector called out "in" when he inserted the needle and "out" when he removed the needle. Infants were videotaped from 5 to 10 s prior to vaccination till three minutes after second vaccination was performed using a handheld video camera. This immunization procedure was standardized in all babies.", "The medical staff that opened the sealed envelope and labeled the syringe with opaque tape was not involved in any other aspect of the study. Neither the doctor injecting the vaccines nor the parents of the infant being vaccinated were aware of the sequence of vaccination. The research assistants, who assessed the infant pain on visualizing the videotapes, were also blinded to the study and treatment allocation.", "Modified Facial Coding System (MFCS) includes five facial expression indicators, viz. brow bulge, eye squeeze, nasolabial fold, mouth open and chin quiver. Truncal movement was added as it supplements evaluation of facial movements in assessment pain. Each indicator was scored from 0 to 1 with 0 indicating absence and 1 indicating its presence. Total composite score was calculated by adding the score of each indicator which ranges from 0 to 6.", "The Neonatal Infant Pain Scale (NIPS) is composed of six indicators: facial expression, cry, breathing patterns, arms, legs and state of arousal. Each behavioral indicator is scored with 0 or 1 except "cry", which has three possible descriptors therefore, being scored with a 0, 1 or 2. Total NIPS pain score is sum of total of score of each indicators. Total score ranges from 0 to 7.", "NIPS and MFCS infant pain scores were assessed prior to 1st and 2nd vaccination, immediately and at 30 s after the 1st vaccination and 2nd vaccination. Also NIPS and MFCS score were assessed at 1 min after 2nd vaccination (Fig. 1 ). Score were assessed by research assistants on visualizing the video. ", "Heart rate (HR) and SpO 2 were recorded prior to vaccination, 30 s after the 1st vaccination and during the 2nd vaccination by pulse oximetry with Schiller Truescope II, (AG, Switzerland) (Fig. 1 ). Infant size pulse oximeter probe was applied to right wrist of all participants.", "Total cry duration was defined as total period of audible distress i.e., start of first cry to cessation of all crying during four minutes period of entire study procedure. It was calculated using stopwatch on viewing the video of the enrolled participants by research assistants.", "The sample size was calculated based on a previous trial done [15] which showed as mean pain score with SD as 8 (1.3) in control ", "Demographic characteristics, clinical and hemodynamic parameters were compared between the two groups using t-test for continuous variable and chi-square test for categorical variable. Wilcoxon rank sum test (Mann-Whitney U-test) was used to analyze non-uniformly distributed data. Mean pain score, mean heart rate and SpO 2 was extrapolated on time line diagram comparing both groups. In addition, area under the curve (AUC) for each parameter from baseline to end of the study was calculated and compared between the two groups. Intragroup analysis was done by paired t-test. The statistical significance level was p < 0.05. All analyses were done using commercial available software program Stata 11, Stata Corp LP, TX, USA.", "Demographic characteristics were comparable in both the groups (Table 1 ). Out of 130 infants, data of 124 infants were available for primary analysis (Fig. 2 ). Video of 6 infants was of faulty quality. Mean MFCS and NIPS score were compared in two groups at time points of study (Table 2 ).", "The mean (SD) of AUC of MFCS and NIPS group was significantly more in DF group as compared with HF group (Table 3 , Fig. 3 ). On intragroup analysis (Table 4 ) Hepatitis B vaccine when injected second after DTwP is more painful than injected first indicated by significantly lesser pain score difference between time points in both MFCS and NIPS. Out of 130 infants enrolled, data of 128 infants were available for analysis of heart rate and SpO 2 (Table 5 ). For two infants, heart rate and SpO 2 could not be recorded during procedure due to malfunction of pulse oximeter.", "The mean (SD) of AUC for heart rate and SpO 2 was significantly more in DF group than as compared in HF group, (Table 3 ). The AUC of heart rate and SpO 2 in DF group was more than HF group by 31.95 (95% CI of 13-50) and by -8.6 (95% CI of -12 to 5).", "For total cry duration, out of 130 infants, data of 124 infants were available for analysis. Median total cry duration was comparable in both HF and DF group (Table 5 ).", "In the present study, effect of change in the sequence of DTwP and Hepatitis B vaccine on pain was evaluated in healthy term infants up to 4 months of age. We observed that giving Hepatitis B vaccination first followed by DTwP vaccine injection in opposite limb resulted in lower pain scores and lesser change in physiological parameters like heart rate and saturation of oxygen (SpO 2 ). Hepatitis B was found to be more painful when injected after DTwP. However, no difference in total cry duration was observed in the two groups.", "There are no previous studies comparing change in measures of pain due to change in order of the two vaccines used in our study. However, other studies have also shown that order of vaccination does affect the pain perceived by the infants. Ipp et al. have reported that mean MBPS score and VAS score were significantly lower in infant receiving quadravalent DTaP-Hib vaccine first than in infant who received pneumococcal conjugate vaccine first [15] . Ravikiran et al. compared pain response on switching the sequence of vaccination of BCG and Hepatitis B vaccine. They demonstrated that pain was significantly lesser when BCG was given first. They measured pain by two commonly used pain score, NIPS and VAS score [24] . They did not use multidimensional measures of pain which probably gives better assessment of pain than evaluating only one pain measure like pain scores. Though other studies [9, 11, 30] have reported duration of cry as sensitive marker of pain, the present study demonstrated no difference in total cry duration in the two groups. Ipp et al. noted that there were fewer infant who cried after first vaccination in DTaP-Hib group but after second vaccine injection occurrence of cry was comparable in the two groups. Recent studies have showed that though commonly used as a surrogate marker, presence of cry may not be good direct sign of pain [2, 23] , rather high pitch and constantly loud siren cry are more typical of pain [3] .", "We used two pain scales, namely Modified Facial Coding System (MFCS) and Neonatal Infant Pain Scale (NIPS) for reliable pain evaluation in our infants. Neonatal facial coding system provides sensitive, reliable and valid measurements of short-term acute pain in both preterm and full-term babies up to 18 months [2, 14, 18] . It has excellent interrater reliability with intraclass correlation of >0.85. Modified Facial Coding System which is a simplified modification of Neonatal Facial Coding System has been used previously by Upadhyay et al. to demonstrate analgesic effect of expressed breast milk in infants during venepuncture [30] . Similarly MFCS was used in other studies to measure infant pain response [9, 11] . NIPS is also a validated pain scale used in various studies to observe pain perception of infants under 12 months of age [1, 6, 7] . This scale also has excellent interrater reliability with intraclass correlation of >0.92. We used multidimensional pain assessment using pain scores, cry duration and physiological parameter. Using multiple parameter for pain assessment have been shown to be more reliable than unidimensional measure of pain [20] .", "DTwP vaccine is more painful than Hepatitis B, possibly due to additive effect of multiple ingredient particularly diphtheria and tetanus toxoid in comparison to Hepatitis B vaccine which has single active ingredient [19] . Ipp et al. hypothesized that relatively higher pain response when more painful vaccine (DTwP) is given earlier than less painful vaccine (Hepatitis B) is possibly due to sensitization of central and peripheral neurons. The sensitized nociceptive system probably has lower threshold for its activation and responses to subsequent inputs are amplified [15, 17] . Whether this hypothesis of lesser painful vaccine first would be applicable to other vaccine combination like MMR and DPT given simultaneously at 18 months is difficult to extrapolate. The strengths of our study were that it was a randomized controlled trial with reasonable sample size. Neither staff nor the physician or research assistant who evaluated the video was aware of the group allotted to infants. We kept approximately one minute gap between two vaccine injections to allow the infant to settle, without unduly prolonging the whole procedure [13] .", "Analgesic was not given during the procedure. There are no current national guidelines for use of analgesic during immunization in India or the state. Some recent studies suggests that prophylactic use of analgesic like paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain may interfere with antibody responses to some antigens [16, 22] . The Cochrane review concluded that though non-pharmacological interventions, such as, non-nutritive sucking, kangaroo care, swaddling/facilitated tucking reduced pain response and touch or rocking/holding were ineffective in reducing pain in old infants of 1 month to 36 months of age [21] . However, there was significant heterogeneity in the studies included in the meta-analysis for having too much confidence in the results. We, therefore, allowed the parents to touch and console their babies after injection in both the groups, which could potentially reduce pain in these infants.", "Though it may potentially affect the outcomes like pain scores and duration of cry, it was thought to be unethical to prevent parents from consoling their crying infants. Also, since this was common in both the groups, it was unlikely to affect the pain outcomes. Other measures of pain detection like salivary cortisol or Near infrared spectroscopy (NIRS) based on hemoglobin concentration in somatosensory area of brain [25] in response to painful stimulus were not measured in this present study.", "We did not look for nociceptive evoke activity in brain and spinal reflex withdrawal activity which are more direct measures of pain activity in infants. Recent study in which EEG and EMG recoding of infant was done after heal lance showed that there was similar nociceptive activity in brain and spinal cord neuron even with administration of oral sucrose [26] .", "Infants experienced lesser pain when Hepatitis B vaccine was given first followed by DTwP instead when DTwP vaccine was given first. Simple change in sequence of administering vaccine is an effective non-pharmacological procedural intervention which results in reducing pain during vaccination of infants."], "domain_risk_levels": ["Low Risk", "Some Concerns", "Low Risk", "Low Risk", "Low Risk"], "overall_risk_level": "Some Concerns", "paper_id": "30966", "pdf_hash": "c9ed91ce392b4ac16a5f796fdd3f0eb8fc3443d5", "result": [{"addedParagraphs": [], "ctxP1Score": "0.560", "ctxP2Score": "0.273", "ctxP3Score": "0.264", "ctxParagraph1": "Infants were randomly allocated to 1 of 2 groups in a 1:1 ratio by block randomization with computer-generated randomly permuted blocks of 8. The numbers were written on small slips and placed in sequentially numbered opaque sealed envelopes (SNOSE METHOD). Random sequence was generated by an independent statistician using online software generated random number list with URL http://www.sealedenvelope.com. The subjects were randomized into two groups:", "ctxParagraph1Feedback": "good", "ctxParagraph2": "The medical staff that opened the sealed envelope and labeled the syringe with opaque tape was not involved in any other aspect of the study. Neither the doctor injecting the vaccines nor the parents of the infant being vaccinated were aware of the sequence of vaccination. The research assistants, who assessed the infant pain on visualizing the videotapes, were also blinded to the study and treatment allocation.", "ctxParagraph2Feedback": null, "ctxParagraph3": "This study was designed as a randomized parallel group active controlled trial. After approval from Institutional Ethical Committee, this study was carried out in the Immunization Clinic of Department of Pediatrics, LLRM Medical College, Meerut, India.", "ctxParagraph3Feedback": null, "domain_number": 1, "expertExplanation": "The infants were randomly allocated to 1 of 2 groups in a 1:1 ratio using block randomization with computer-generated randomly permuted blocks of 8. The random sequence was generated by an independent statistician using online software. Therefore, the allocation sequence was random.", "expertPrediction": "yes", "gptExplanation": "The infants were randomly allocated to 1 of 2 groups in a 1:1 ratio using block randomization with computer-generated randomly permuted blocks of 8. The random sequence was generated by an independent statistician using online software. Therefore, the allocation sequence was random.", "gptPrediction": "yes", "gptResponse": "{
